/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/aws-saa.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("aws-saa", [["saa1",0,1,"An application on EC2 instances must read objects from one S3 bucket. The security team bans long-term access keys on servers. What should the architect do?",["Attach an IAM role with read access to that bucket through an instance profile","Store an IAM user's access keys in the application's configuration file","Add the instances' private IP addresses as grantees in the bucket's ACL","Pass an IAM user's access keys to the instances through EC2 user data at every launch"],0,"An instance profile delivers a role's temporary credentials through the instance metadata service and rotates them automatically. Keys in a config file or user data are long-term secrets that can leak, and bucket ACLs cannot grant access by IP address.","1.1 Secure access to AWS resources",[null,"Access keys in a config file are exactly the long-term credentials the security team banned, and they can leak through backups or source control.","S3 ACLs grant access to AWS accounts or predefined groups, not to IP addresses, and ACLs are disabled by default on new buckets.","User data still delivers long-term IAM user keys, and anyone who can view the instance's user data can read them."],2],["saa2",0,1,"An IAM policy attached to a developer allows s3:DeleteObject on a bucket, but a service control policy on the developer's account denies s3:DeleteObject. What happens when the developer tries to delete an object?",["The request is allowed, because identity policies are evaluated before SCPs","The request is denied, because an SCP deny is an explicit deny that overrides any allow","The request is allowed only if the bucket policy also grants the action","The request is denied only when it is made by the account's root user"],1,"In IAM policy evaluation an explicit deny anywhere wins, and SCPs set the maximum permissions for every principal in a member account, including its root user. A bucket policy allow cannot override an SCP deny.","1.1 Secure access to AWS resources",["Order does not matter in IAM evaluation: every applicable policy is gathered, and an explicit deny anywhere, including an SCP, wins.",null,"A bucket policy allow cannot override an SCP deny; SCPs cap what any principal in the member account can do.","SCPs apply to every principal in a member account, not only the root user, so the developer is denied too."],2],["saa3",0,1,"A company runs 40 AWS accounts in AWS Organizations. Nobody in the member accounts, including account administrators, may stop or delete the CloudTrail trail. What is the most effective control?",["An IAM permissions boundary attached to every administrator in each account","A CloudTrail setting that turns on log file integrity validation for the organization trail","An AWS Config rule that reports when a trail stops logging in any account","An SCP on the organization's OUs that denies the CloudTrail stop and delete actions"],3,"An SCP applies to every principal in the affected accounts and cannot be bypassed from inside them. Permissions boundaries must be set on each principal and admins could remove them, log validation only detects tampering with log files, and a Config rule only reports after the fact.","1.1 Secure access to AWS resources",["Boundaries must be attached to every principal one by one, and account administrators could remove or change them.","Log file integrity validation only detects altered or deleted log files after the fact; it does not stop anyone from stopping the trail.","A Config rule reports that logging stopped but does not prevent the action, so a gap in logging would still occur.",null],3],["saa4",0,1,"Employees already sign in to a corporate SAML 2.0 identity provider. They need single sign-on to the console and CLI of 25 AWS accounts with role-based permissions. What should the architect set up?",["An IAM user with the same name and password in each of the 25 accounts","A shared IAM user per team, with its password kept in a password manager","IAM Identity Center connected to the corporate IdP, with permission sets assigned to accounts","An Amazon Cognito user pool that holds a copy of every employee's password"],2,"IAM Identity Center federates with an external IdP and provisions permission sets as roles in each account, giving short-term credentials for the console and CLI. Per-account IAM users multiply credentials, shared users break accountability, and Cognito user pools are for application end users.","1.1 Secure access to AWS resources",["Separate IAM users in 25 accounts multiply long-term credentials and bypass the corporate IdP the employees already use.","Shared users break accountability because actions can no longer be tied to one person, and they still bypass the IdP.",null,"Cognito user pools are for application end users, and copying employee passwords into another store defeats federation."],2],["saa5",0,1,"A mobile app lets users sign in with a social identity provider and then upload photos straight to S3, each user only under their own prefix. Which design is most secure?",["An IAM user's access keys compiled into the mobile app so every upload is signed","A bucket policy that allows public writes, with a Lambda function that renames each object","A separate IAM user created for every person who signs up in the mobile app","A Cognito identity pool that exchanges the login token for temporary credentials scoped by a policy variable"],3,"Cognito identity pools trade an identity provider token for temporary, limited AWS credentials, and a policy variable such as the Cognito identity ID restricts each user to their own prefix. Embedded keys can be extracted, public write is unsafe, and IAM users do not scale to consumers.","1.1 Secure access to AWS resources",["Keys compiled into an app can be extracted by anyone who downloads it, giving them long-term access to the bucket.","Public write access lets anyone on the internet upload content, and renaming objects afterwards does nothing to stop abuse.","IAM users are meant for a small number of workforce identities, not millions of consumers, and each would need long-term keys.",null],3],["saa6",0,1,"Which statement correctly compares security groups and network ACLs in a VPC?",["Security groups are stateless and evaluate rules in number order from the lowest","Network ACLs are stateful, so return traffic is always allowed back automatically","Security groups are stateful and allow-only; network ACLs are stateless and can allow or deny","Security groups work at the subnet boundary and can contain explicit deny rules"],2,"Security groups attach to network interfaces, are stateful and only have allow rules. Network ACLs attach to subnets, are stateless (return traffic needs its own rule) and evaluate numbered allow and deny rules in order.","1.2 Secure workloads and applications",["This describes network ACLs, not security groups: security groups are stateful and evaluate all their rules together.","Network ACLs are stateless, so return traffic needs its own explicit rule, often for the ephemeral port range.",null,"Security groups attach to network interfaces, not subnets, and they support only allow rules."],1],["saa7",0,1,"Security analysts find that a specific /24 address range is scanning web servers in a public subnet. The range must be blocked immediately while all other internet users keep access. What should the architect change?",["Add an inbound deny rule for the range to the security group attached to the web servers","Remove the internet gateway route from the public subnet's route table","Add a deny rule for the range, with a low rule number, to the subnet's network ACL","Move the web servers into a spread placement group in the same subnet"],2,"Network ACLs support explicit deny rules evaluated in number order, so a low-numbered deny blocks the range for the whole subnet. Security groups have no deny rules, and removing the internet gateway route cuts off every user.","1.2 Secure workloads and applications",["Security groups only support allow rules, so there is no way to add a deny for a specific range.","Removing the internet gateway route would cut off every internet user, not just the scanning range.",null,"Placement groups control physical placement of instances and have no effect on which IP addresses can reach them."],2],["saa8",0,1,"Application servers in private subnets must download operating system patches from the internet, but nothing on the internet may start a connection to them. What should the architect add?",["An Elastic IP address attached directly to each application server in the subnets","A default route to the internet gateway in each private subnet's route table","A NAT gateway in a public subnet, with a default route to it from the private route tables","An interface VPC endpoint that points to the operating system vendor's repository"],2,"A NAT gateway lets private instances start outbound connections while blocking inbound ones from the internet. Elastic IPs or an internet gateway route would make the subnets public, and interface endpoints only reach AWS and PrivateLink services, not arbitrary internet sites.","1.2 Secure workloads and applications",["An Elastic IP in a subnet with an internet gateway route makes the server reachable from the internet, which the requirement forbids.","A default route to the internet gateway turns the private subnets into public ones, allowing inbound connections to instances with public IPs.",null,"Interface endpoints reach AWS services and PrivateLink endpoint services, not arbitrary internet patch repositories."],2],["saa9",0,1,"Administrators need shell access to Linux instances in private subnets. The company wants no open inbound ports, no bastion host to patch, and every session logged. What should the architect use?",["A bastion host in a public subnet with TCP port 22 open to 0.0.0.0/0","An internet-facing Network Load Balancer that forwards TCP port 22 to the instances","A Site-to-Site VPN from each administrator's laptop to the virtual private gateway","AWS Systems Manager Session Manager, with the SSM Agent and an instance profile on each instance"],3,"Session Manager connects through the SSM Agent's outbound connection, needs no inbound ports or bastion, and can log sessions to CloudWatch Logs or S3. An open bastion or NLB exposes SSH to the internet, and per-laptop Site-to-Site VPNs are not how remote users connect.","1.2 Secure workloads and applications",["A bastion open to 0.0.0.0/0 on port 22 exposes SSH to the whole internet and still has to be patched and managed.","An internet-facing NLB forwarding port 22 publishes SSH to the internet, which is the opposite of no open inbound ports.","Site-to-Site VPN connects networks with a customer gateway device, not individual laptops, and it would not log shell sessions.",null],3],["saa10",0,1,"Instances in private subnets write sensitive files to S3. Policy says this traffic must not cross the public internet or pass through the NAT gateway. What should the architect add?",["A second NAT gateway placed in another Availability Zone of the VPC","S3 Transfer Acceleration turned on for the destination bucket","An internet gateway with a route for the S3 managed prefix list in each private subnet","An S3 gateway VPC endpoint associated with the private subnets' route tables"],3,"A gateway endpoint adds a route for the S3 prefix list so traffic stays on the AWS network without a NAT gateway, and an endpoint policy or aws:sourceVpce condition can restrict it further. Transfer Acceleration uses public edge locations, and another NAT gateway still sends traffic through NAT.","1.2 Secure workloads and applications",["A second NAT gateway improves availability, but traffic still goes through NAT, which the policy forbids.","Transfer Acceleration routes traffic through public CloudFront edge locations to speed long-distance transfers; it does not keep traffic private.","An internet gateway is the path to the public internet, and private subnets would need NAT or public IPs to use it anyway.",null],2],["saa11",0,1,"Which pair of AWS services can be reached from a VPC through gateway VPC endpoints?",["Amazon SQS and Amazon SNS","Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB","AWS KMS and AWS Secrets Manager","Amazon ECR and AWS STS"],1,"Only S3 and DynamoDB offer gateway endpoints, which are route table entries with no hourly charge. SQS, SNS, KMS, Secrets Manager, ECR and STS are reached privately with interface endpoints (PrivateLink), which have hourly and per-GB charges.","1.2 Secure workloads and applications",["SQS and SNS are reached privately with interface endpoints (PrivateLink), not gateway endpoints.",null,"KMS and Secrets Manager only offer interface endpoints, which have hourly and per-GB charges.","ECR and STS are also interface endpoint services; gateway endpoints exist only for S3 and DynamoDB."],1],["saa12",0,1,"A static website is served from an S3 bucket through a CloudFront distribution. Users must not be able to bypass CloudFront and fetch objects from S3 directly. What should the architect do?",["Use CloudFront origin access control and a bucket policy that only allows that distribution","Turn on S3 static website hosting and publish the bucket's website endpoint","Keep the bucket public but give every object a long random key name","Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration and require the accelerate endpoint for reads"],0,"Origin access control signs CloudFront's requests to S3, and the bucket policy allows only that distribution, so the bucket can stay private with Block Public Access on. Website hosting and public objects expose the bucket directly, and Transfer Acceleration is about upload speed.","1.2 Secure workloads and applications",[null,"The website endpoint is a public HTTP endpoint that requires public read access, so users could reach the bucket directly.","Random key names are obscurity, not access control; anyone who learns a key can still read the public object.","Transfer Acceleration speeds up long-distance transfers to S3 and does nothing to restrict who can read the bucket."],2],["saa13",0,1,"A web application behind an Application Load Balancer is hit by SQL injection attempts, and one client IP sometimes sends thousands of requests per minute. What should the architect deploy?",["AWS Shield Standard, which already protects every ALB at no extra charge","Amazon GuardDuty with automatic blocking of the offending client IP addresses at the ALB","Security group rules that match SQL keywords in the incoming HTTP requests","An AWS WAF web ACL on the ALB with managed rule groups and a rate-based rule"],3,"AWS WAF inspects HTTP requests at layer 7: managed rules catch SQL injection and a rate-based rule throttles noisy IPs. Shield Standard covers network and transport layer DDoS, GuardDuty detects threats but does not filter HTTP, and security groups cannot inspect request contents.","1.2 Secure workloads and applications",["Shield Standard protects against network and transport layer DDoS attacks; it does not inspect HTTP requests for SQL injection.","GuardDuty detects threats and produces findings, but it does not filter HTTP traffic or block IPs at the ALB by itself.","Security groups filter only by protocol, port and source; they cannot read HTTP request contents such as SQL keywords.",null],3],["saa14",0,1,"A company that sells tickets online is worried about large DDoS attacks during launches. It wants 24/7 access to the AWS Shield Response Team and protection against scaling charges caused by an attack. Which service meets this?",["AWS Shield Standard","AWS Firewall Manager","Amazon Inspector","AWS Shield Advanced"],3,"Shield Advanced adds enhanced detection, the Shield Response Team, cost protection for attack-driven scaling, and AWS WAF at no extra charge for protected resources. Shield Standard is free and automatic but has none of those, Firewall Manager centrally manages rules, and Inspector scans for vulnerabilities.","1.2 Secure workloads and applications",["Shield Standard is free and automatic but includes neither Shield Response Team access nor DDoS cost protection.","Firewall Manager centrally manages WAF, Shield and security group policies across accounts; it is not the DDoS protection tier itself.","Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities and has nothing to do with DDoS mitigation.",null],1],["saa15",0,1,"Auditors require that S3 data be encrypted with a key whose use is logged in CloudTrail, whose key policy the company controls, and that can be disabled to cut off access. Which option meets all three needs?",["SSE-S3 with keys managed entirely by Amazon S3","SSE-C with a key the client sends on every request","SSE-KMS with the AWS managed aws/s3 key","SSE-KMS with a customer managed key in AWS KMS"],3,"A customer managed KMS key gives the company its own key policy, CloudTrail records of each use, and the ability to disable or schedule deletion of the key. SSE-S3 offers no key control, the AWS managed key's policy cannot be edited, and SSE-C leaves key management and logging entirely to the client.","1.3 Data security controls",["With SSE-S3, S3 manages the keys, so the company has no key policy to control and cannot disable the key.","SSE-C keys are supplied by the client on each request and are not managed in KMS, so there is no key policy or KMS usage log.","The aws/s3 AWS managed key logs usage, but its key policy cannot be edited and it cannot be disabled by the customer.",null],3],["saa16",0,1,"What does envelope encryption mean when AWS services use AWS KMS?",["Every object is sent to KMS, which encrypts the full object and returns it","Data is encrypted twice, first with an AES-256 key and then again with an RSA key pair","The KMS key is stored alongside the data inside an encrypted archive file","Data is encrypted with a data key, and that data key is encrypted under a KMS key"],3,"KMS generates a data key; the service encrypts the data locally with the plaintext data key, then stores only the encrypted copy of the data key next to the data. KMS keys never leave KMS unencrypted, and sending whole objects to KMS would hit the 4 KB direct-encryption limit.","1.3 Data security controls",["KMS encrypts at most 4 KB directly, so services do not send whole objects to KMS; they encrypt locally with a data key.","Envelope encryption is about wrapping a data key with a KMS key, not about encrypting data twice with two different algorithms.","KMS keys never leave KMS unencrypted; only the encrypted data key is stored with the data.",null],1],["saa17",0,1,"A bucket stores medical reports. Compliance says any request that does not use TLS must be rejected. What should the architect add?",["Default bucket encryption set to SSE-KMS with a customer managed key and a bucket key","A bucket policy that denies all actions when aws:SecureTransport is false","S3 Block Public Access turned on at the account level","S3 Object Lock in compliance mode with a one-year retention period"],1,"The aws:SecureTransport condition key is false for plain HTTP requests, so a deny on that condition enforces encryption in transit. Default encryption protects data at rest, Block Public Access controls public grants, and Object Lock prevents deletion; none of them block HTTP.","1.3 Data security controls",["Default encryption protects data at rest; it does not stop clients from sending requests over plain HTTP.",null,"Block Public Access prevents public grants but says nothing about whether authorized requests use TLS.","Object Lock prevents versions from being deleted or overwritten; it does not reject non-TLS requests."],2],["saa18",0,1,"An application stores its RDS database password in plain text in a configuration file. Security wants the password rotated every 30 days automatically and never stored in code. What should the architect use?",["AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation, with the application fetching the secret at runtime","A Systems Manager Parameter Store String parameter that an engineer updates by hand at the start of each month","An environment variable set in EC2 user data and changed whenever instances are replaced","Automatic yearly rotation turned on for the KMS key that encrypts the RDS storage volume"],0,"Secrets Manager has built-in rotation for RDS credentials using a rotation Lambda function, and applications read the current value by API. A plain String parameter is neither encrypted nor rotated, user data is visible to anyone who can describe the instance, and KMS key rotation does not change the database password.","1.3 Data security controls",[null,"A String parameter is not encrypted, and manual monthly updates are neither automatic nor reliable rotation.","User data and environment variables are readable by anyone who can describe the instance, and nothing rotates the password.","KMS key rotation changes the key material protecting the storage volume; the database password stays the same."],2],["saa19",0,1,"Which statement about AWS Secrets Manager and Systems Manager Parameter Store is correct?",["Parameter Store charges per secret per month, while Secrets Manager standard parameters are free","Secrets Manager has managed rotation for supported databases; Parameter Store has no built-in rotation","Only Parameter Store can encrypt values with AWS KMS; Secrets Manager stores them as plain text","Secrets Manager values can be read only from Lambda functions, not from EC2 or containers"],1,"Secrets Manager encrypts with KMS, bills per secret, and offers managed rotation. Parameter Store standard parameters are free, SecureString parameters are encrypted with KMS, but rotation needs your own automation. Both are read through the API from any compute service.","1.3 Data security controls",["This reverses the pricing: Parameter Store standard parameters have no extra charge, while Secrets Manager charges per secret.",null,"Secrets Manager always encrypts secrets with KMS, and Parameter Store also uses KMS for SecureString values.","Both services are read through AWS APIs, so EC2 instances, containers and Lambda functions can all use them."],1],["saa20",0,1,"Regulations require that trade records in S3 cannot be overwritten or deleted by anyone, including the root user, for seven years. What should the architect configure?",["S3 Object Lock in compliance mode with a seven-year retention period","S3 Object Lock in governance mode with a seven-year retention period","S3 Versioning with a lifecycle rule that expires versions after seven years","A bucket policy that denies s3:DeleteObject to every IAM principal"],0,"Compliance mode is write-once-read-many: no user, including root, can shorten retention or delete a locked version. Governance mode can be bypassed by users with special permission, versioning alone allows version deletion, and a bucket policy can be edited by an administrator.","1.3 Data security controls",[null,"Governance mode can be bypassed by users with the s3:BypassGovernanceRetention permission, so it does not stop everyone.","Versioning keeps old versions, but users with permission can still delete specific versions, and a lifecycle rule does not lock anything.","A bucket policy can be edited or removed by an administrator or the root user, so it is not an immutable control."],2],["saa21",0,1,"A partner must download one private report from S3 for the next hour without receiving AWS credentials. What is the simplest secure option?",["Generate a presigned URL for the object that expires in one hour","Make the object public and remove the public ACL one hour later","Create an IAM user for the partner and delete it the next day","Copy the object to a new bucket with S3 Block Public Access turned off"],0,"A presigned URL carries the signer's permission for one object and expires at a set time, and the bucket stays private. Public objects, extra buckets or throwaway IAM users all widen access and depend on someone remembering to clean up.","1.3 Data security controls",[null,"A public object is readable by anyone who finds it during that hour, and someone has to remember to reverse it.","An IAM user issues AWS credentials to the partner, which the requirement excludes, and it remains active until someone deletes it.","Turning off Block Public Access on a copy widens exposure and still does not give the partner a way to authenticate."],1],["saa22",0,1,"A company has hundreds of S3 buckets and must find which ones contain personal data such as passport numbers and credit card numbers. Which service fits best?",["AWS Audit Manager","Amazon Inspector","Amazon Macie","Amazon Detective"],2,"Macie uses managed and custom data identifiers to discover sensitive data in S3 and reports findings per bucket. Audit Manager collects evidence for compliance audits, Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities, and Detective helps investigate security findings.","1.3 Data security controls",["Audit Manager collects evidence and maps controls for compliance audits; it does not scan S3 objects for personal data.","Inspector scans EC2, ECR images and Lambda for software vulnerabilities, not the contents of S3 objects.",null,"Detective helps investigate the root cause of security findings; it does not discover sensitive data."],1],["saa23",0,1,"Which service continuously analyzes CloudTrail events, VPC Flow Logs and DNS query logs to detect threats such as an instance communicating with a known crypto-mining server?",["Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Security Hub","AWS Config","AWS Trusted Advisor"],0,"GuardDuty is a managed threat detection service that reads these log sources without agents and produces findings. Security Hub collects and scores findings from GuardDuty and other services but does not analyze the logs itself, Config tracks resource configuration and compliance, and Trusted Advisor checks accounts against best practices.","1.2 Secure workloads and applications",[null,"Security Hub aggregates and scores findings from GuardDuty and others but does not analyze the raw logs itself.","Config records resource configuration history and compliance, not network or DNS activity that would reveal crypto-mining.","Trusted Advisor checks accounts against best practices such as open ports and idle resources; it is not a threat detector."],1],["saa24",0,1,"The security team must be told whenever any security group in the account allows SSH from 0.0.0.0/0, and needs a history of how each security group has changed. What should the architect set up?",["Amazon Inspector network reachability scans run once a quarter against each EC2 instance","AWS Config with a managed rule for unrestricted SSH and an EventBridge notification","Amazon Macie discovery jobs that run against every bucket in the account","CloudWatch Logs Insights queries run by hand against the application logs"],1,"AWS Config records configuration history for each resource and evaluates managed rules such as restricted-ssh continuously; a noncompliant result can trigger an EventBridge rule. Quarterly scans are not continuous, Macie looks at S3 data, and application logs do not show security group changes.","1.2 Secure workloads and applications",["Inspector network reachability findings are not a configuration history, and quarterly scans are not continuous notification.",null,"Macie discovers sensitive data in S3 buckets and knows nothing about security group rules.","Application logs do not record security group changes, and manual queries are neither continuous nor a configuration history."],2],["saa25",0,1,"An application in account B must read items from a DynamoDB table in account A. What is the recommended way to grant access?",["Create an IAM user in account A and copy its access keys into account B","Share the root user credentials of account A with the team in account B","Create a role in account A that trusts account B, and have the application assume it with STS","Peer the VPCs of both accounts so the application can reach the table directly"],2,"Cross-account roles give temporary credentials: account A's role has a trust policy for account B and a permissions policy for the table, and account B's principal needs sts:AssumeRole. Copied keys and shared root credentials are long-term secrets, and VPC peering provides network paths, not authorization.","1.1 Secure access to AWS resources",["Copying IAM user access keys shares long-term credentials across accounts that can leak and must be rotated by hand.","Sharing root credentials gives unlimited access to account A and violates every AWS security best practice.",null,"VPC peering provides a network path, but DynamoDB access is authorized by IAM, and the table is reached through a public or endpoint API anyway."],2],["saa26",0,1,"Developers must be allowed to create IAM roles for their Lambda functions, but no role they create may ever have more permissions than a set the security team defines. What should the architect use?",["An S3 bucket policy on the bucket that stores the Lambda deployment packages","A resource-based policy on each Lambda function that lists the allowed actions","A permissions boundary that developers are required to attach to every role they create","An AWS Config rule with remediation that deletes new roles once a week if they look too powerful"],2,"A permissions boundary caps the maximum permissions of a role, and an IAM condition can force developers to attach it when creating roles. Bucket and function policies do not limit what roles can do, and a weekly clean-up leaves a window of excess access.","1.1 Secure access to AWS resources",["A bucket policy controls access to that bucket's objects; it cannot limit the permissions of IAM roles developers create.","A Lambda resource-based policy controls who can invoke the function, not what the function's execution role is allowed to do.",null,"A weekly clean-up is detective and leaves days in which an overly powerful role can be used."],3],["saa27",0,1,"A public web application behind an Application Load Balancer needs an HTTPS certificate for www.example.com that renews automatically. What should the architect do?",["Request a public certificate in AWS Certificate Manager and attach it to an HTTPS listener","Create a self-signed certificate and import it into IAM for the load balancer","Install a certificate on every instance and renew each one by hand yearly","Store the certificate's private key in an S3 bucket that the ALB can read"],0,"ACM issues free public certificates, validates them by DNS or email, and renews them automatically when they are in use on integrated services such as ALB and CloudFront. Self-signed certificates are not trusted by browsers, and manual per-instance renewal is error-prone.","1.3 Data security controls",[null,"Browsers do not trust self-signed certificates, and IAM certificate storage does not renew anything automatically.","Certificates on each instance must be renewed by hand, which is error-prone, and the ALB terminates TLS anyway.","An ALB does not read certificates from S3, and storing a private key in a bucket is a needless exposure risk."],1],["saa28",0,2,"A web application runs on a single large EC2 instance in one Availability Zone. The business wants it to survive the loss of an instance or an entire AZ. What should the architect do?",["Move the application to a larger instance type with more CPU and memory","Put the instance in a cluster placement group and take hourly EBS snapshots","Run instances in an Auto Scaling group across two or more AZs behind an Application Load Balancer","Attach a second EBS volume and mirror the data between the two volumes"],2,"Spreading instances across AZs behind a load balancer removes the single point of failure, and the Auto Scaling group replaces unhealthy instances. A bigger instance or extra volume is still one instance in one AZ, and a cluster placement group packs instances close together, which is the opposite of spreading risk.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["A larger instance is still one instance in one AZ, so a single failure or AZ outage still takes the application down.","A cluster placement group packs instances together in one AZ, which increases correlated risk, and snapshots only help after an outage.",null,"Mirroring two volumes on one instance in one AZ does nothing if the instance or the AZ fails."],2],["saa29",0,2,"Instances in an Auto Scaling group behind an ALB sometimes return HTTP 500 errors while their EC2 status checks still pass, and the group never replaces them. What should the architect change?",["Increase the group's desired capacity by two instances","Switch the load balancer to a Network Load Balancer","Lower the Auto Scaling cooldown period to 60 seconds","Turn on ELB health checks for the Auto Scaling group"],3,"By default an Auto Scaling group only uses EC2 status checks. With ELB health checks on, instances that fail the load balancer's application health check are marked unhealthy and replaced. More capacity or a different load balancer leaves broken instances in service.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["More instances add capacity, but the broken instances remain in service because nothing marks them unhealthy.","An NLB does not fix the problem; the Auto Scaling group still ignores load balancer health unless ELB health checks are enabled.","The cooldown period affects how often scaling policies act, not whether unhealthy instances are detected and replaced.",null],3],["saa30",0,2,"An order website receives bursts of traffic during sales. The back-end processing tier cannot keep up, and some orders are lost when it is overloaded. What design change fixes this?",["Increase the front-end instance count so it retries orders more often","Put an SQS queue between the tiers and scale the workers on queue depth","Have the front end write every order straight to the database synchronously","Move the processing tier into a single larger EC2 instance type"],1,"A queue decouples the tiers: the front end writes orders quickly, messages stay durable until processed, and workers scale on ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible. More retries add load, synchronous writes keep the tight coupling, and one bigger instance is still a bottleneck and single point of failure.","2.1 Scalable and loosely coupled architectures",["More front-end instances retrying orders only add load to the overwhelmed back end and can still lose orders.",null,"Synchronous writes keep the tiers tightly coupled, so bursts still overload the database and processing path.","One larger instance raises the ceiling a little but remains a bottleneck and a single point of failure."],2],["saa31",0,2,"A payments system must process each customer's transactions exactly once and in the order they were sent, while different customers can be processed in parallel. What should the architect use?",["An SQS standard queue with long polling turned on for the consumers","An SNS standard topic with one subscription for each customer","An SQS FIFO queue with the customer ID as the message group ID","A Kinesis data stream with a single shard for every customer"],2,"FIFO queues give ordering within a message group and deduplication, and different group IDs can be processed in parallel. Standard queues give at-least-once delivery with best-effort ordering, SNS standard topics do not guarantee order, and a single shard serializes all customers.","2.1 Scalable and loosely coupled architectures",["Standard queues deliver at least once with best-effort ordering, so duplicates and reordering can happen.","SNS standard topics do not guarantee ordering or exactly-once delivery, and a subscription per customer does not scale.",null,"Kinesis orders records per shard, but creating a shard per customer is wasteful and consumers must still handle duplicate records."],3],["saa32",0,2,"A few malformed messages in an SQS queue fail processing again and again, blocking workers. How should the architect handle them?",["Set the queue's visibility timeout to zero so messages come back sooner","Configure a dead-letter queue with a redrive policy and a maxReceiveCount","Turn on long polling with a 20-second wait time on the source queue","Change the source queue's message retention period to the minimum of 60 seconds"],1,"After a message has been received maxReceiveCount times without being deleted, SQS moves it to the dead-letter queue for inspection. A zero visibility timeout causes duplicate processing, long polling only reduces empty receives, and short retention silently drops good messages too.","2.1 Scalable and loosely coupled architectures",["A zero visibility timeout makes messages reappear immediately, so they are processed repeatedly and duplicates increase.",null,"Long polling reduces empty receive responses and cost; it does nothing about messages that keep failing.","A 60-second retention period deletes good messages too if they are not processed quickly, silently losing orders."],2],["saa33",0,2,"When an order is placed, three independent systems must react: billing, shipping and analytics. Each must get every order even if one of them is down for an hour. What is the best design?",["Send each order to a single SQS queue that all three systems poll","Have the web tier call the three systems' APIs one after another","Write each order to an S3 bucket that each system lists every hour","Publish to an SNS topic that fans out to a separate SQS queue for each system"],3,"SNS fan-out delivers a copy to each subscribed queue, and each queue buffers messages while its consumer is down. With one shared queue each message goes to only one consumer, and chained API calls fail when any system is unavailable.","2.1 Scalable and loosely coupled architectures",["With one shared queue each message is delivered to only one consumer, so each system would see only some of the orders.","Sequential API calls couple the web tier to all three systems, so one system being down makes orders fail or go missing.","Hourly listing of a bucket adds delay and custom tracking of which orders each system has already processed.",null],3],["saa34",0,2,"An insurance claim workflow has several steps, retries on failure, and waits up to five days for a human approval before continuing. Which service should coordinate it?",["A single Lambda function that runs the whole process","An SQS delay queue that holds the claim for five days","AWS Step Functions with a Standard workflow","A cron job on an EC2 instance that polls a database"],2,"Standard workflows can run for up to a year, have built-in retry and catch, and support callback task tokens for human approval. Lambda stops after 15 minutes, SQS delay queues cap at 15 minutes, and a cron job on one instance is a single point of failure you must build and maintain.","2.1 Scalable and loosely coupled architectures",["A single Lambda invocation can run for at most 15 minutes, far short of a five-day approval wait.","SQS delay queues can postpone delivery by at most 15 minutes, and they provide no retries, branching or workflow state.",null,"A cron job on one instance is a single point of failure and leaves you to build retries and state tracking yourself."],2],["saa35",0,2,"A team wants to run containerized microservices on AWS without provisioning, patching or scaling any EC2 instances. What should the architect recommend?",["Amazon ECS tasks on the EC2 launch type with an Auto Scaling group","Amazon EKS with self-managed node groups on EC2 instances","Docker installed on EC2 instances managed with Systems Manager","Amazon ECS tasks that use the AWS Fargate launch type"],3,"Fargate runs containers on serverless capacity, so there are no hosts to patch or scale. The EC2 launch type and self-managed EKS nodes both leave the team responsible for the instances, as does running Docker on EC2.","2.1 Scalable and loosely coupled architectures",["With the EC2 launch type the team still provisions, patches and scales the container instances in the Auto Scaling group.","Self-managed node groups are EC2 instances the team must patch, upgrade and scale.","Running Docker directly on EC2 leaves the team responsible for the hosts and for orchestration.",null],2],["saa36",0,2,"An application uses Amazon RDS for MySQL in one Availability Zone. It must fail over automatically if that AZ fails, without changing the connection string. What should the architect do?",["Create a read replica in the same Region and point writes to it","Take automated snapshots every hour and restore on failure","Change to a larger instance class with Provisioned IOPS storage","Convert the database to a Multi-AZ deployment"],3,"Multi-AZ keeps a synchronous standby in another AZ and fails over automatically by updating the DNS endpoint the application already uses. A read replica needs manual promotion and a new endpoint, snapshots mean downtime and data loss, and a bigger instance is still in one AZ.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["A read replica uses asynchronous replication, needs manual promotion, and has its own endpoint, so the connection string would change.","Restoring from snapshots means downtime and data loss since the last snapshot, and the restored instance gets a new endpoint.","A larger instance with faster storage is still a single instance in one AZ, so an AZ failure still causes an outage.",null],2],["saa37",0,2,"What is the main purpose of an Amazon RDS read replica, compared with the standby of a Multi-AZ DB instance?",["To provide synchronous replication for automatic failover","To store automated backups outside the primary Region","To serve read traffic from an asynchronously updated copy","To encrypt the primary database with a separate key"],2,"Read replicas use asynchronous replication and accept read queries, which offloads reporting and read-heavy traffic; they can be promoted manually. The standby in a classic Multi-AZ DB instance deployment is synchronous, used for failover and does not serve reads.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["Synchronous replication for automatic failover describes the Multi-AZ standby, not a read replica, which replicates asynchronously.","Automated backups can be replicated to another Region, but that is a backup feature, not the purpose of read replicas.",null,"Encryption is set with a KMS key on the instance; replicas are not an encryption mechanism."],1],["saa38",0,2,"A MySQL-compatible application has users on three continents. It needs low-latency local reads in each Region, an RPO of about one second, and failover to another Region in about a minute. What should the architect use?",["Amazon Aurora Global Database","An RDS for MySQL Multi-AZ DB cluster in one Region","Nightly RDS snapshots copied to each of the Regions","DynamoDB global tables in three of the Regions"],0,"Aurora Global Database replicates storage to secondary Regions with typical lag under a second, serves local reads there, and a secondary can be promoted in about a minute. A Multi-AZ cluster stays in one Region, snapshot copies mean hours of data loss, and DynamoDB is not MySQL-compatible.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",[null,"A Multi-AZ DB cluster protects against AZ failure within one Region; it gives no local reads or failover on other continents.","Nightly snapshots mean up to a day of data loss and hours to restore, far from a one-second RPO and one-minute failover.","DynamoDB global tables are a NoSQL key-value store, so a MySQL-compatible application would need to be rewritten."],3],["saa39",0,2,"A gaming company needs a NoSQL table that accepts writes in both us-east-1 and eu-west-1 at the same time, so players in each Region get low-latency writes. What should the architect use?",["DynamoDB global tables with replicas in both Regions","A DynamoDB table with point-in-time recovery turned on","A DynamoDB table and a DAX cluster in each of the two Regions","DynamoDB Streams that copy items to an S3 bucket in the other Region"],0,"Global tables are multi-Region, multi-active: each replica accepts reads and writes and changes replicate to the others. Point-in-time recovery protects against mistakes, DAX only caches reads, and streaming into S3 does not give a writable second table.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",[null,"Point-in-time recovery restores a table to an earlier moment; it does not create a writable copy in another Region.","DAX caches reads for a table in one Region; it does not replicate writes between Regions.","Items copied into S3 are not a writable DynamoDB table, so players in the other Region would get no low-latency writes."],2],["saa40",0,2,"A script accidentally overwrote thousands of items in a DynamoDB table 20 minutes ago. The team wants the table as it was 25 minutes ago. Which feature allows this if it was already enabled?",["DynamoDB Streams","Time to Live","Point-in-time recovery","Auto scaling"],2,"Point-in-time recovery lets you restore a table to any second in the last 35 days (the window is configurable), into a new table. Streams record 24 hours of changes but do not restore anything, TTL deletes expired items, and auto scaling adjusts capacity.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["Streams hold item-level changes for 24 hours for consumers to process, but they provide no restore operation.","Time to Live deletes items after an expiry timestamp; it cannot bring back overwritten data.",null,"Auto scaling adjusts provisioned read and write capacity and has nothing to do with data recovery."],2],["saa41",0,2,"A company runs identical stacks in two Regions. All users should go to the primary Region and be sent to the secondary only when the primary's health check fails. Which Route 53 configuration fits?",["Weighted routing with 50% of traffic going to each Region","Latency-based routing between the two Regions' load balancers","Geolocation routing based on the country of each user","Failover routing with a health check on the primary record"],3,"Failover routing answers with the primary record while its health check is healthy and switches to the secondary when it fails. Weighted and latency routing send traffic to both Regions all the time, and geolocation routes by user location, not by health.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["A 50/50 weighted split sends half of all users to the secondary Region all the time, not only when the primary fails.","Latency-based routing sends each user to the lowest-latency Region, so both Regions serve traffic continuously.","Geolocation routing chooses by user location, so it would split users between Regions rather than keeping them on the primary.",null],2],["saa42",0,2,"Which Route 53 routing policy should an architect use to send 10% of users to a new version of an application and 90% to the current one?",["Failover routing","Multivalue answer routing","Weighted routing","Geoproximity routing"],2,"Weighted routing splits DNS answers by the relative weights on each record, which is how canary releases are done at the DNS level. Failover is active-passive, multivalue returns several healthy records at random, and geoproximity routes by location and bias.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["Failover routing is active-passive and sends all traffic to one record unless its health check fails; it cannot split by percentage.","Multivalue answer returns up to eight healthy records at random, with no control over the traffic percentage.",null,"Geoproximity routes by the geographic distance between users and resources, adjusted with a bias, not by fixed percentages."],1],["saa43",0,2,"An internal reporting system can tolerate losing up to 24 hours of data and being down for a day. The company wants the lowest-cost disaster recovery strategy. Which strategy fits?",["Pilot light, with the database replicating to another Region","Backup and restore, with backups copied to another Region","Warm standby, with a smaller copy running in another Region","Multi-site active-active across two AWS Regions"],1,"With an RPO and RTO measured in hours to a day, backup and restore is enough and costs the least, because nothing runs in the recovery Region until needed. Pilot light, warm standby and active-active give faster recovery at progressively higher running cost.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["Pilot light keeps a database replicating in another Region all the time, which costs more than this tolerance requires.",null,"Warm standby runs a scaled-down copy of the whole stack continuously, which is far more than a one-day RTO needs.","Active-active runs full production in two Regions, the most expensive option, for a system that can be down a day."],2],["saa44",0,2,"An e-commerce platform needs an RTO of under 15 minutes after a Regional outage. The company accepts paying for a reduced-size copy of the full stack running all the time, but not for a full-size second Region. Which DR strategy matches?",["Backup and restore","Pilot light","Warm standby","Multi-site active-active"],2,"Warm standby keeps a scaled-down but fully working environment running, so recovery is mainly scaling it up. Pilot light keeps only core components such as the database replicating, so servers must be launched first. Backup and restore is slower still, and active-active runs full capacity in both Regions.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["Backup and restore needs infrastructure rebuilt and data restored, typically taking hours, which misses a 15-minute RTO.","Pilot light keeps only the data layer live, so servers must be launched and scaled first, which is usually slower than warm standby.",null,"Active-active runs full-size capacity in a second Region, which the company explicitly does not want to pay for."],3],["saa45",0,2,"A company uses a pilot light DR design: an Aurora replica runs in the recovery Region, but no application servers run there. During a Regional outage, what must happen before the recovery Region can serve production traffic?",["Restore the database from the most recent nightly snapshot in that Region","Launch the application tier from AMIs, promote the database and switch DNS","Nothing, because full-size application servers already take traffic there","Copy every EBS snapshot from the failed Region into the recovery Region"],1,"In pilot light only the core data layer is live, so recovery means starting and scaling the application tier, promoting the replica to a writer, and pointing DNS at the new Region. Restoring from snapshots describes backup and restore, full-size servers already serving traffic describes active-active, and snapshots cannot be copied out of a failed Region on demand.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["Restoring from snapshots describes backup and restore; in pilot light the Aurora replica already holds current data.",null,"Full-size servers already serving traffic describes active-active or hot standby, not pilot light, where no servers run.","Snapshots stored only in the failed Region cannot be copied during its outage, and pilot light relies on the live replica instead."],3],["saa46",0,2,"A company must back up EBS volumes, RDS databases, DynamoDB tables and EFS file systems across 12 accounts on one schedule, keep them for 90 days, and copy them to a second Region. What should the architect use?",["An EventBridge rule that calls a snapshot Lambda in each account","Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager policies for all of the resources","AWS Backup plans managed through AWS Organizations","S3 Cross-Region Replication rules for each of the data stores"],2,"AWS Backup centralizes schedules, retention and cross-Region and cross-account copies for many services, and backup policies can be applied across the organization. Custom Lambda code must be built and maintained, Data Lifecycle Manager covers only EBS snapshots and AMIs, and S3 replication only applies to S3.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["Custom Lambda code in 12 accounts must be written, secured and maintained, and it would have to handle every service's API.","Data Lifecycle Manager only manages EBS snapshots and EBS-backed AMIs, so RDS, DynamoDB and EFS would not be covered.",null,"Cross-Region Replication copies S3 objects only; it cannot back up EBS, RDS, DynamoDB or EFS."],2],["saa47",0,2,"An architect wants new objects in an S3 bucket copied automatically to a bucket in another Region for disaster recovery. What must be enabled before S3 Cross-Region Replication can be configured?",["S3 Transfer Acceleration on the source bucket","S3 Object Lock on the destination bucket","S3 Versioning on both the source and destination buckets","Requester Pays on both of the buckets"],2,"Replication requires versioning on the source and destination buckets, plus an IAM role that S3 can use to replicate. Transfer Acceleration, Object Lock and Requester Pays are unrelated to whether replication can be set up.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["Transfer Acceleration speeds up long-distance client transfers and is not a prerequisite for replication.","Object Lock can be used with replication but is not required; versioning is the prerequisite.",null,"Requester Pays changes who pays for requests and transfer; it is unrelated to setting up replication."],1],["saa48",0,2,"A company's data center connects to AWS over a single AWS Direct Connect link. It needs a backup path if that link fails, at the lowest cost. What should the architect add?",["A second Direct Connect link at the same Direct Connect location","An AWS Site-to-Site VPN over the internet as a backup path","A VPC peering connection between the data center and the VPC","A CloudFront distribution that points at the on-premises servers"],1,"A Site-to-Site VPN is quick to set up and inexpensive, and BGP can prefer Direct Connect while falling back to the VPN. A second Direct Connect link gives higher resilience but costs much more, VPC peering only connects VPCs, and CloudFront is for content delivery.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["A second Direct Connect link is the most resilient option but costs much more, and at the same location it shares that location as a failure point.",null,"VPC peering connects two VPCs; it cannot connect an on-premises data center to AWS.","CloudFront is a content delivery network for serving content to users; it is not a private network path to a data center."],2],["saa49",0,2,"A company has 30 VPCs across several accounts and two data centers connected by VPN. Every network must reach every other one, and managing a full mesh of peering connections has become unworkable. What should the architect use?",["More VPC peering connections, with transitive routing turned on","A single NAT gateway shared by all 30 VPCs and both data centers","An Application Load Balancer in each VPC in front of all traffic","AWS Transit Gateway as a regional hub for the VPCs and VPNs"],3,"Transit Gateway is a hub-and-spoke router: each VPC and VPN attaches once and route tables control what can talk to what. VPC peering is never transitive, a NAT gateway only translates outbound traffic, and an ALB handles HTTP requests, not routing between networks.","2.1 Scalable and loosely coupled architectures",["VPC peering is never transitive and has no option to turn transitive routing on, so a full mesh would still be needed.","A NAT gateway translates outbound traffic from private subnets to the internet; it does not route between VPCs and data centers.","An ALB distributes HTTP requests to targets; it does not provide network routing between VPCs and on-premises networks.",null],3],["saa50",0,2,"After a Regional outage drill, a team found that rebuilding the network, load balancers and Auto Scaling groups by hand in the recovery Region took two days. How can they cut this time most reliably?",["Write a detailed runbook of console steps and practice it every year","Take EBS snapshots of every instance and copy them to the recovery Region","Define the environment in AWS CloudFormation templates and deploy them to the recovery Region","Buy a larger AWS Support plan so engineers can open cases faster"],2,"Infrastructure as code builds the same environment repeatably and quickly in any Region, and StackSets can deploy to many accounts and Regions. Runbooks still rely on manual steps, snapshots only cover disks, not the network or load balancers, and a support plan does not rebuild anything.","2.2 Highly available and fault-tolerant architectures",["A runbook still depends on people clicking through consoles correctly under pressure, which is slow and error-prone.","Snapshots cover disk data only; the network, load balancers and Auto Scaling groups would still be built by hand.",null,"A higher support plan gives faster help from AWS, but engineers would still rebuild the environment by hand."],3],["saa51",0,3,"A tightly coupled HPC simulation runs on 16 EC2 instances that exchange data constantly and need the lowest possible network latency between nodes. How should the instances be placed?",["In a spread placement group across three Availability Zones","In a partition placement group with one partition per node","In a cluster placement group in a single Availability Zone","In separate Auto Scaling groups in two different Regions"],2,"A cluster placement group packs instances close together in one AZ for low latency and high throughput, and supports Elastic Fabric Adapter on supported types. Spread and partition groups place instances on separate hardware for resilience, which adds distance, and crossing Regions adds much more latency.","3.2 High-performing compute solutions",["A spread group puts each instance on separate hardware, and spreading across AZs adds latency between the nodes.","A partition group separates instances into different racks for fault isolation, which adds distance instead of minimizing latency.",null,"Splitting the cluster across Regions adds far more latency, and tightly coupled nodes need to be close together."],2],["saa52",0,3,"Five instances run a critical quorum-based service. The company wants to minimize the chance that one hardware failure takes out more than one instance. Which placement strategy fits?",["A cluster placement group","A spread placement group","A dedicated Auto Scaling warm pool","One larger bare metal instance"],1,"A spread placement group puts each instance on distinct racks with separate power and network, up to seven per AZ per group. A cluster group packs instances close together, a warm pool only speeds up scaling, and one bare metal instance is a single point of failure.","3.2 High-performing compute solutions",["A cluster group packs instances close together, possibly on shared racks, so one failure can take out several nodes.",null,"A warm pool keeps pre-initialized instances ready to speed up scale-out; it does not control hardware placement.","One bare metal instance puts the whole quorum on a single host, the largest possible single point of failure."],2],["saa53",0,3,"A self-managed database on EC2 needs 150,000 sustained IOPS with consistent sub-millisecond latency on a single volume. Which EBS volume type should the architect choose?",["Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2 Block Express)","General Purpose SSD (gp3)","General Purpose SSD (gp2)","Cold HDD (sc1)"],0,"io2 Block Express supports up to 256,000 IOPS per volume with sub-millisecond latency and 99.999% durability. gp3 and gp2 cannot reach 150,000 IOPS on one volume, and sc1 is a low-cost HDD built for infrequent sequential access, not random IOPS.","3.1 High-performing and scalable storage",[null,"A gp3 volume's maximum IOPS is well below 150,000, so one gp3 volume cannot meet the requirement.","gp2 ties IOPS to size and tops out far below 150,000 IOPS per volume, with less consistent latency.","sc1 is a cold HDD for infrequently accessed sequential data and delivers very low random IOPS."],2],["saa54",0,3,"A log-processing job reads multi-terabyte files sequentially from EBS every night. Throughput matters, IOPS do not, and cost should be low. Which volume type fits best?",["Throughput Optimized HDD (st1)","Provisioned IOPS SSD (io1)","Amazon FSx for NetApp ONTAP","EC2 instance store NVMe"],0,"st1 is a low-cost HDD volume designed for large sequential reads and writes such as big data and log processing. io1 costs far more per GB for IOPS the job does not need, FSx for NetApp ONTAP is a managed multi-protocol file system rather than a cheap block volume, and instance store data is lost when the instance stops.","3.1 High-performing and scalable storage",[null,"io1 charges for provisioned IOPS that this sequential job does not need, making it far more expensive per GB.","FSx for NetApp ONTAP is a managed multi-protocol file system, not a low-cost block volume for a single job.","Instance store data is lost when the instance stops or terminates, and it only exists on some instance types."],2],["saa55",0,3,"Linux web servers in an Auto Scaling group across three Availability Zones must share the same set of uploaded files through a POSIX file system that grows automatically. What should the architect use?",["Amazon EFS mounted on every instance","An EBS volume attached with Multi-Attach","The instance store on each web server","An FSx for Lustre scratch file system"],0,"EFS is a managed NFS file system that many Linux instances across AZs can mount at once, and it grows and shrinks automatically. EBS Multi-Attach is limited to io1 and io2 volumes in one AZ and needs a cluster-aware file system, instance store is not shared or durable, and a Lustre scratch file system is a fixed-size, single-AZ file system for short-lived HPC data.","3.1 High-performing and scalable storage",[null,"EBS Multi-Attach works only for io1 and io2 volumes within a single AZ and needs a cluster-aware file system.","Each instance store is local to one host and is not shared, and its data is lost when instances stop or terminate.","A Lustre scratch file system is a fixed-size, single-AZ file system for temporary HPC data, not a shared web content store."],1],["saa56",0,3,"A company is moving Windows applications that use SMB file shares, NTFS permissions and Active Directory authentication. What is the best managed storage choice?",["Amazon EFS with the Standard storage class","Amazon FSx for Windows File Server","Amazon FSx for Lustre linked to S3","Amazon S3 with S3 Access Points"],1,"FSx for Windows File Server provides native SMB shares, NTFS ACLs and Active Directory integration. EFS is NFS for Linux, FSx for Lustre is for high-performance compute, and S3 is object storage, not an SMB file system.","3.1 High-performing and scalable storage",["EFS is an NFS file system for Linux clients; it does not provide SMB shares, NTFS ACLs or Active Directory integration.",null,"FSx for Lustre is a high-performance parallel file system for Linux HPC and ML, not a Windows SMB file server.","S3 is object storage accessed over HTTPS; Access Points simplify access policies but do not provide SMB or NTFS permissions."],1],["saa57",0,3,"A machine learning team trains models on a 200 TB data set stored in S3 and needs a file system with very high throughput and low latency for the compute cluster during each training run. What should the architect choose?",["Amazon EFS in the One Zone storage class","An S3 bucket mounted as a shared drive","Amazon FSx for Lustre linked to the S3 bucket","EBS gp3 volumes on each training instance"],2,"FSx for Lustre is built for HPC and ML, delivers hundreds of GB/s, and can lazy-load and write back to a linked S3 bucket. EFS is general-purpose NFS with lower peak throughput, S3 is not a POSIX file system, and separate gp3 volumes are not shared.","3.1 High-performing and scalable storage",["EFS One Zone is a lower-cost general-purpose NFS file system with much lower peak throughput than Lustre.","S3 is object storage, not a POSIX file system, so mounting it as a drive gives limited semantics and performance for training.",null,"Separate gp3 volumes are not shared, and each would need its own copy of the 200 TB data set."],3],["saa58",0,3,"Users around the world upload video files of several gigabytes to one S3 bucket in us-east-1. Uploads from Asia and Europe are slow. What should the architect do to improve upload speed?",["Turn on S3 Transfer Acceleration and use multipart uploads","Create a CloudFront distribution with the bucket as its origin","Move the bucket to the S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access class","Put a Network Load Balancer in front of the S3 bucket"],0,"Transfer Acceleration sends uploads to the nearest edge location and over the AWS backbone to the bucket, and multipart upload sends parts in parallel and retries only failed parts. CloudFront mainly speeds up downloads, a storage class does not change network speed, and an NLB cannot front S3.","3.1 High-performing and scalable storage",[null,"CloudFront mainly accelerates downloads of cached content; uploads through it are not the standard fix for slow S3 uploads.","One Zone-IA is a storage class that changes cost and resilience, not network transfer speed.","A Network Load Balancer cannot be placed in front of an S3 bucket; S3 is reached through its own endpoints."],2],["saa59",0,3,"Which statement about S3 multipart upload is correct?",["Parts upload in parallel, and it is required for objects larger than 5 GB","It is required for all objects larger than 100 MB","It only works when Transfer Acceleration is turned on","Each part must be at least 1 GB, except the last part"],0,"A single PUT can upload up to 5 GB, so larger objects need multipart upload; AWS recommends it from about 100 MB. Parts can be 5 MB to 5 GB, can upload in parallel, and work with or without Transfer Acceleration.","3.1 High-performing and scalable storage",[null,"AWS recommends multipart upload from about 100 MB, but it is only required above the 5 GB single PUT limit.","Multipart upload works with or without Transfer Acceleration; the two features are independent.","Parts can be as small as 5 MB (except the last part); there is no 1 GB minimum."],1],["saa60",0,3,"An application on RDS for PostgreSQL runs the same product catalog queries thousands of times a minute, and database CPU is near 90%. The data changes a few times a day. What is the most effective way to reduce latency and load?",["Convert the database to a Multi-AZ instance so the standby handles half the queries","Increase the allocated storage so the volume gets more baseline IOPS","Move the database to a larger instance and turn on storage autoscaling","Add Amazon ElastiCache for Redis and cache query results with lazy loading"],3,"A cache serves repeated reads in microseconds and removes them from the database; lazy loading fills the cache on a miss, and a TTL keeps data fresh. A classic Multi-AZ standby does not serve reads, more storage IOPS does not fix CPU, and a larger instance costs more without removing repeated work.","3.3 High-performing database solutions",["The standby of a classic Multi-AZ instance does not serve reads, so it cannot take half of the queries.","More storage can add baseline IOPS, but the bottleneck is CPU from repeated queries, not disk I/O.","A larger instance raises cost and headroom but still runs the same repeated queries thousands of times a minute.",null],3],["saa61",0,3,"A DynamoDB table serves a read-heavy leaderboard. The team needs microsecond read latency with minimal code changes. What should the architect add?",["DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","DynamoDB Streams","A global secondary index","ElastiCache for Memcached"],0,"DAX is an in-memory cache built for DynamoDB and is API-compatible, so the application mainly switches to the DAX client. Streams capture changes, a GSI gives another query pattern but not microsecond reads, and Memcached would need custom caching logic in the application.","3.3 High-performing database solutions",[null,"Streams capture a time-ordered log of item changes for consumers; they do not speed up reads.","A GSI adds another query pattern with its own key, but reads still take DynamoDB's usual single-digit milliseconds.","Memcached would need custom cache-aside code in the application and is not API-compatible with DynamoDB."],1],["saa62",0,3,"A news website hosted in one Region serves images, videos and HTML to readers worldwide, and readers far from the Region see slow page loads. What should the architect do first?",["Add more EC2 instances to the website's Auto Scaling group","Move the website's origin to a larger instance type in the same Region","Put an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of the website","Create a Route 53 weighted record for the existing load balancer"],2,"CloudFront caches content at edge locations close to readers, cutting latency and load on the origin, and it also speeds up dynamic content over the AWS network. More or larger origin instances do not shorten the distance to readers, and weighted routing does not add locations.","3.4 High-performing network architectures",["More instances in the same Region add capacity but do not shorten the network distance to readers far away.","A larger origin instance speeds up processing slightly but leaves the long round trips to distant readers unchanged.",null,"A weighted record for one load balancer still sends everyone to the same Region, so distant readers stay slow."],2],["saa63",0,3,"A multiplayer game uses UDP and needs two static IP addresses that clients can hard-code, plus fast failover between game servers in two Regions. What should the architect use?",["Amazon CloudFront with the game servers in both Regions as custom origins","Route 53 latency routing with a TTL of 60 seconds","An Application Load Balancer in each of the two Regions","AWS Global Accelerator with endpoint groups in both Regions"],3,"Global Accelerator provides two static anycast IPs, supports TCP and UDP, routes over the AWS network and fails over between Regions in seconds without waiting for DNS caches. CloudFront handles HTTP and HTTPS, DNS failover depends on client caching, and ALBs are regional HTTP load balancers.","3.4 High-performing network architectures",["CloudFront serves HTTP and HTTPS content and does not proxy arbitrary UDP game traffic or offer two fixed IPs.","DNS-based failover depends on resolvers and clients honoring the TTL, and it does not give clients static IP addresses to hard-code.","ALBs are regional HTTP and HTTPS load balancers; they do not handle UDP and their IP addresses are not static.",null],3],["saa64",0,3,"Hundreds of concurrent Lambda functions connect to an RDS for MySQL database, and the database runs out of connections during traffic spikes. What should the architect add?",["A read replica for every 100 concurrent Lambda functions","An SQS queue that stores the database connection strings","A larger EBS volume attached to the database instance","Amazon RDS Proxy between the functions and the database"],3,"RDS Proxy pools and shares connections, so many short-lived functions reuse a small number of database connections, and it also speeds up failover. Read replicas do not help write connections, a queue of connection strings does nothing, and storage size does not change connection limits.","3.3 High-performing database solutions",["Read replicas spread read queries but do not pool connections, and write connections would still exhaust the primary.","Storing connection strings in a queue does nothing to reduce the number of open database connections.","Connection limits depend on the instance's memory and engine settings, not on the size of the storage volume.",null],3],["saa65",0,3,"A retailer wants to run complex SQL analytics with large joins across several petabytes of sales history and feed BI dashboards. Which service is designed for this?",["Amazon RDS for MySQL","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon ElastiCache","Amazon Redshift"],3,"Redshift is a columnar, massively parallel data warehouse for analytic SQL at petabyte scale. RDS is an OLTP database that struggles at this size, DynamoDB is a key-value store without joins, and ElastiCache is an in-memory cache.","3.3 High-performing database solutions",["RDS for MySQL is a row-based OLTP database that is not built for petabyte-scale analytic joins.","DynamoDB is a key-value store with no joins and no support for complex analytic SQL across petabytes.","ElastiCache is an in-memory cache for fast lookups, not a place to store and analyze petabytes of history.",null],1],["saa66",0,3,"Analysts want to run occasional SQL queries on application logs already stored in S3 as Parquet files, without loading them into a database or managing servers. What should the architect recommend?",["Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL with a nightly import of the log files","Amazon Athena with tables defined in the AWS Glue Data Catalog","Amazon EMR on a cluster of EC2 instances that runs all the time","Amazon Redshift provisioned clusters loaded with the COPY command"],1,"Athena is serverless, queries data in place in S3 with standard SQL and charges per data scanned, which columnar Parquet keeps small. Nightly imports and always-on clusters add servers and cost for occasional queries.","3.5 Data ingestion and transformation",["Nightly imports into RDS add a server to manage, delay the data by a day, and cost more for occasional queries.",null,"An always-running EMR cluster means managing and paying for servers even when no one is querying.","A provisioned Redshift cluster requires loading data and paying for the cluster all the time, which is overkill for occasional queries."],2],["saa67",0,3,"A website must capture clickstream events in real time. Three separate applications need to read the same events, and one of them must be able to reprocess the last 24 hours after a bug fix. Which service fits?",["Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon SQS standard queue","Amazon Data Firehose","Amazon SNS standard topic"],0,"Kinesis Data Streams keeps records for 24 hours by default (extendable) so multiple consumers can read independently and replay from a point in time. An SQS message is deleted once processed, Firehose delivers to destinations without replay, and SNS pushes messages without storing them.","3.5 Data ingestion and transformation",[null,"An SQS message is consumed by one consumer and deleted after processing, so three apps cannot each read every event or replay it.","Firehose delivers data to destinations such as S3 and does not keep records for multiple consumers to replay.","SNS pushes each message to subscribers once and does not store messages, so there is nothing to reprocess later."],2],["saa68",0,3,"A company wants streaming application logs delivered to S3 in near real time, converted to Parquet, with no servers or consumer code to manage. What should the architect use?",["Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with a custom consumer on EC2","Amazon Data Firehose with record format conversion to S3","Amazon SQS with a Lambda function that writes files","AWS DataSync scheduled to run every 15 minutes"],1,"Firehose is a fully managed delivery service that buffers, optionally transforms and converts to Parquet or ORC, and writes to S3. Data Streams and SQS need consumer code, and DataSync copies files between storage systems rather than ingesting streams.","3.5 Data ingestion and transformation",["Data Streams needs consumer code, and running it on EC2 means managing servers, which the requirement rules out.",null,"SQS with Lambda requires custom code to batch messages into files and convert them to Parquet.","DataSync copies files between storage systems on a schedule; it does not ingest streaming records."],1],["saa69",0,3,"An internal application gets a sharp rise in traffic every weekday at 8:45 a.m. when staff sign in. Instances take several minutes to boot, so users see slow responses first thing. What should the architect add?",["A simple scaling policy that adds one instance when CPU passes 90%","A scheduled scaling action that raises capacity shortly before 8:45 a.m.","A longer health check grace period on the Auto Scaling group","A larger minimum group size set by hand every morning by the operations team"],1,"A scheduled action adds capacity before the known peak, so instances are warm when users arrive (predictive scaling can also learn the pattern). A reactive CPU policy starts adding instances only after users are already affected, and manual changes are error-prone.","3.2 High-performing compute solutions",["A reactive CPU policy only adds instances after load rises, and with slow boots users are already affected.",null,"The health check grace period delays health checks on new instances; it does not add capacity before the peak.","Manual daily changes are error-prone and depend on someone remembering, when a scheduled action does it automatically."],2],["saa70",0,3,"Which EC2 Auto Scaling policy type adds or removes instances to keep a chosen metric, such as average CPU utilization, close to a set value like 50%?",["Scheduled scaling","Simple scaling","Manual scaling","Target tracking scaling"],3,"Target tracking works like a thermostat: it creates and manages CloudWatch alarms to hold the metric near the target. Scheduled scaling changes capacity at set times, simple scaling applies one adjustment per alarm, and manual scaling needs a person to change capacity.","3.2 High-performing compute solutions",["Scheduled scaling changes capacity at set times; it does not respond to a metric at all.","Simple scaling applies one fixed adjustment per alarm and then waits for a cooldown, rather than holding a metric at a target.","Manual scaling means a person changes the desired capacity; nothing keeps the metric near a value automatically.",null],1],["saa71",0,3,"A company must move 80 TB of archive files to S3 within two weeks. Its internet link is 100 Mbps and already busy during business hours. What should the architect recommend?",["Copy the files over the internet with AWS DataSync","Upload the files with S3 Transfer Acceleration","Order AWS Snowball Edge devices and ship the data to AWS","Set up a new Site-to-Site VPN and use the AWS CLI"],2,"At 100 Mbps, 80 TB takes more than 70 days even with the link fully used, so an offline Snowball transfer is the only way to meet two weeks. DataSync, Transfer Acceleration and a VPN all still depend on the same slow link.","3.5 Data ingestion and transformation",["DataSync still uses the same 100 Mbps link, and 80 TB would take more than 70 days even at full speed.","Transfer Acceleration speeds up long-distance routing but cannot exceed the 100 Mbps local link, so it is still far too slow.",null,"A VPN runs over the same internet link, adds encryption overhead, and does not increase the available bandwidth."],2],["saa72",0,3,"A company is migrating an on-premises Oracle database to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL and can accept only a few minutes of downtime at cutover. What should the architect use?",["AWS DMS alone, with a full load during a weekend outage window","AWS SCT to convert the schema, then AWS DMS with change data capture","Oracle Data Pump export files copied to S3 and then imported into Aurora","AWS DataSync to copy the Oracle data files to an EFS file system"],1,"A heterogeneous migration needs the Schema Conversion Tool for schema and code, and DMS with ongoing replication keeps the target in sync until a short cutover. DMS alone does not convert Oracle-specific schema objects, Data Pump files cannot be imported into PostgreSQL, and copying data files does not convert anything.","3.5 Data ingestion and transformation",["DMS alone does not convert Oracle-specific schema objects and code to PostgreSQL, and a weekend full load means long downtime.",null,"Data Pump files are an Oracle-specific format that Aurora PostgreSQL cannot import.","Copying raw Oracle data files to EFS converts nothing; PostgreSQL cannot read Oracle data files."],2],["saa73",0,4,"A company runs a steady 24/7 workload and expects to keep that level of compute for three years, but it plans to move parts of it from EC2 to Fargate and Lambda and may change Regions. Which purchase option saves the most while allowing this?",["EC2 Instance Savings Plans for the current family","Standard Reserved Instances for the current type","On-Demand Capacity Reservations in each AZ","A Compute Savings Plan with a three-year term"],3,"Compute Savings Plans apply across instance families, sizes, Regions, operating systems, Fargate and Lambda. EC2 Instance Savings Plans and Standard RIs are tied to a family or type in one Region, and Capacity Reservations guarantee capacity without any discount on their own.","4.2 Cost-optimized compute solutions",["EC2 Instance Savings Plans are tied to one instance family in one Region and do not cover Fargate or Lambda.","Standard RIs are tied to an instance type or family in one Region and do not apply to Fargate or Lambda.","Capacity Reservations guarantee capacity in an AZ but give no discount on their own and bill whether used or not.",null],3],["saa74",0,4,"A media company renders video frames in batch jobs that can stop and resume at any time, and deadlines are flexible. Which EC2 purchasing option is cheapest?",["On-Demand Instances","Dedicated Instances","Spot Instances","Standard Reserved Instances"],2,"Spot offers spare capacity at large discounts, and interruptions are acceptable because the jobs checkpoint and resume. On-Demand costs more, Dedicated Instances add a premium for isolated hardware, and RIs need a one- or three-year commitment to steady use.","4.2 Cost-optimized compute solutions",["On-Demand has no interruptions, but its price is much higher than Spot for work that can tolerate interruption.","Dedicated Instances add a premium for single-tenant hardware, which a render job does not need.",null,"Standard RIs require a one- or three-year commitment to steady usage, which does not fit flexible batch jobs."],2],["saa75",0,4,"How much warning does Amazon EC2 give before it reclaims a Spot Instance?",["A two-minute interruption notice","A 30-second interruption notice","A 15-minute rebalance window","A one-hour termination notice"],0,"EC2 sends a two-minute Spot interruption notice through instance metadata and EventBridge. A separate rebalance recommendation can arrive earlier, but it is a signal, not a guaranteed time window.","4.2 Cost-optimized compute solutions",[null,"EC2 gives two minutes of warning, not 30 seconds, through instance metadata and EventBridge.","There is no 15-minute rebalance window; a rebalance recommendation is an early signal with no guaranteed time.","EC2 does not give a one-hour termination notice for Spot Instances; the warning is two minutes."],1],["saa76",0,4,"A company is moving licensed software to EC2. The license is priced per physical CPU socket and core, so the company must see and control the underlying server. Which option meets this?",["EC2 Dedicated Hosts","EC2 Dedicated Instances","Spot Instances in a cluster placement group","Compute Savings Plans"],0,"Dedicated Hosts give a whole physical server with visibility of sockets and cores, supporting bring-your-own-license terms. Dedicated Instances run on single-tenant hardware but hide the host details, and Spot or Savings Plans are pricing models, not host visibility.","4.2 Cost-optimized compute solutions",[null,"Dedicated Instances run on single-tenant hardware but do not expose sockets and cores or let you control placement on the host.","Spot Instances are an interruptible pricing model and give no visibility of the physical server's sockets and cores.","Compute Savings Plans are a pricing commitment, not a way to see or control the underlying physical server."],2],["saa77",0,4,"A development environment of 20 EC2 instances is used only from 8 a.m. to 6 p.m. on weekdays. What is the most cost-effective approach?",["Stop the On-Demand instances outside working hours with a schedule","Buy three-year All Upfront Reserved Instances for every one of the instances","Move each instance onto its own EC2 Dedicated Host","Buy a Compute Savings Plan sized for 24 hours of use a day"],0,"The instances are needed about 50 hours out of 168 a week, so stopping them the rest of the time (for example with AWS Instance Scheduler) cuts compute cost by around 70%. Reservations and Savings Plans bill for every hour of the term whether used or not, and Dedicated Hosts add cost.","4.2 Cost-optimized compute solutions",[null,"Reserved Instances bill for every hour of the term, so reserving for 24/7 when instances run about 50 hours a week wastes money.","Dedicated Hosts add cost for whole physical servers, which a development environment does not need.","A Savings Plan sized for 24 hours a day commits to spend for hours when the instances are not needed."],2],["saa78",0,4,"A data lake bucket holds objects whose access patterns are unknown and change over time. The company wants automatic savings without retrieval fees or lifecycle rules to maintain. Which storage class fits?",["S3 Standard-Infrequent Access","S3 Intelligent-Tiering","S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access","S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval"],1,"Intelligent-Tiering moves each object between access tiers based on its own access, with a small monitoring fee and no retrieval fees. Standard-IA and One Zone-IA charge retrieval fees and suit data you know is rarely read, and Glacier Flexible Retrieval takes minutes to hours to restore.","4.1 Cost-optimized storage solutions",["Standard-IA charges per-GB retrieval fees and suits data known to be rarely read, not unknown patterns.",null,"One Zone-IA also charges retrieval fees and stores data in one AZ, and it does not adapt to changing access.","Glacier Flexible Retrieval requires restores that take minutes to hours, which does not fit data that may be read at any time."],2],["saa79",0,4,"Application logs in S3 are read often for 30 days, occasionally for the next 60 days, and never after one year, when they can be deleted. How should the architect keep storage costs down with the least effort?",["Run a nightly Lambda function that copies logs into a new bucket","Add an S3 Lifecycle configuration that transitions and then expires the objects","Turn on S3 Versioning so older logs move to cheaper storage","Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration to compress the log objects"],1,"A lifecycle rule can move objects to Standard-IA after 30 days, to a Glacier class after 90 days, and delete them after 365 days, with no code. Custom Lambda copies cost more to build and run, versioning keeps more data rather than less, and Transfer Acceleration does not compress anything.","4.1 Cost-optimized storage solutions",["A custom Lambda copy job costs more to build and run, and copying into a new bucket does not delete or tier anything by itself.",null,"Versioning keeps old versions, which increases storage; it does not move objects to cheaper classes.","Transfer Acceleration speeds up long-distance transfers; it does not compress objects or change storage class."],2],["saa80",0,4,"A company keeps thumbnail images that can be regenerated from the originals at any time. They are read rarely but must load in milliseconds when requested. Which storage class is the cheapest suitable choice?",["S3 Standard","S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access","S3 Glacier Deep Archive","S3 Standard-Infrequent Access"],1,"One Zone-IA costs less than Standard-IA because it stores data in one AZ; losing that AZ is acceptable when the data can be recreated, and access is still in milliseconds. Standard costs more for rarely read data, and Deep Archive takes hours to restore.","4.1 Cost-optimized storage solutions",["S3 Standard costs more to store than the infrequent access classes, which is wasteful for rarely read images.",null,"Deep Archive retrievals take hours, so images could not load in milliseconds.","Standard-IA stores data across multiple AZs, which costs more than One Zone-IA and is unnecessary for recreatable data."],3],["saa81",0,4,"A company has hundreds of gp2 EBS volumes that are larger than needed only to get more IOPS. How can the architect lower cost without downtime?",["Change the volumes to sc1 Cold HDD to cut the price per GB","Change the volumes to gp3 with Elastic Volumes and set IOPS separately","Take snapshots, delete the volumes and restore them from the snapshots only at night","Move the data to instance store volumes on larger instances"],1,"gp3 is cheaper per GB than gp2, includes 3,000 IOPS and 125 MB/s baseline at any size, and lets you buy extra IOPS without buying capacity; Elastic Volumes changes type online. Cold HDD cannot deliver the IOPS, deleting volumes breaks the workload, and instance store is not persistent.","4.1 Cost-optimized storage solutions",["sc1 is a cold HDD with very low IOPS, so it cannot deliver the performance these volumes were sized for.",null,"Deleting volumes and restoring them only at night takes the workload offline and creates downtime.","Instance store data is lost when instances stop or terminate, and moving to it requires migrating to new instances."],2],["saa82",0,4,"Regulators require bank records to be kept for ten years. They are almost never read, and a retrieval time of up to 48 hours is acceptable. Which S3 storage class is the lowest cost?",["S3 Glacier Deep Archive","S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval","S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval","S3 Intelligent-Tiering"],0,"Deep Archive is the lowest-cost S3 class, with standard retrieval within 12 hours and bulk within 48 hours. Instant Retrieval and Flexible Retrieval cost more to store, and Intelligent-Tiering is aimed at changing access patterns.","4.1 Cost-optimized storage solutions",[null,"Glacier Instant Retrieval offers millisecond access at a higher storage price, which is unnecessary when 48 hours is acceptable.","Glacier Flexible Retrieval costs more to store than Deep Archive, and its faster retrievals are not needed here.","Intelligent-Tiering adds monitoring fees and targets changing access patterns, not known long-term archives."],1],["saa83",0,4,"A new DynamoDB table backs an event ticketing app. Traffic is near zero most days and spikes unpredictably to thousands of requests per second when tickets go on sale. Which capacity setup is most cost-effective?",["On-demand capacity mode","Provisioned capacity sized for the peak","Provisioned capacity with reserved capacity","A DAX cluster in front of a small table"],0,"On-demand bills per request and scales instantly, so idle days cost almost nothing and spikes need no planning. Provisioning for the peak pays for unused capacity most of the time, reserved capacity needs steady use, and DAX only caches reads, not writes during sales.","4.3 Cost-optimized database solutions",[null,"Provisioning for the peak means paying for thousands of unused capacity units on the many near-idle days.","Reserved capacity is a commitment to steady provisioned throughput, which this spiky, mostly idle table does not have.","DAX caches reads only, so it does not help the write-heavy ticket sales, and it adds a cluster that runs all the time."],2],["saa84",0,4,"A hospital archives medical images that are opened about once a quarter, but when a doctor opens one it must load within milliseconds. Which S3 storage class is most cost-effective?",["S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval with expedited retrievals","S3 Glacier Deep Archive with standard retrievals","S3 Standard with S3 Storage Lens monitoring","S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval"],3,"Glacier Instant Retrieval is for long-lived data accessed about once a quarter, with millisecond access and lower storage cost than Standard-IA. Expedited Flexible Retrieval still takes 1 to 5 minutes and Deep Archive takes hours, while Standard costs more for rarely read data and Storage Lens only reports on usage.","4.1 Cost-optimized storage solutions",["Expedited retrievals from Glacier Flexible Retrieval typically take 1 to 5 minutes, not milliseconds, and cost extra.","Deep Archive standard retrievals take up to 12 hours, far too slow for a doctor waiting on an image.","S3 Standard costs more to store rarely accessed data, and Storage Lens only reports on usage without reducing cost.",null],3],["saa85",0,4,"A test team uses an Aurora MySQL database for a few hours at a time on irregular days, with load that varies widely. The company wants to stop paying for a fixed instance size. What should the architect choose?",["An Aurora provisioned cluster with a three-year reserved instance","Aurora Serverless v2, which scales capacity with the load","An RDS for MySQL Multi-AZ DB cluster with Provisioned IOPS","A self-managed MySQL server running on a Dedicated Host"],1,"Aurora Serverless v2 adjusts capacity in fine steps (ACUs) as load changes, and you pay for the capacity used. A three-year reservation commits to a fixed size, and Multi-AZ or Dedicated Host options add cost for an intermittent test workload.","4.3 Cost-optimized database solutions",["A three-year reservation commits to a fixed instance size for years, the opposite of paying only for what is used.",null,"A Multi-AZ DB cluster with Provisioned IOPS adds standby instances and I/O costs that an intermittent test database does not need.","A self-managed server on a Dedicated Host is more expensive and adds patching work, and it still has a fixed size."],2],["saa86",0,4,"EC2 instances in private subnets read and write large volumes of DynamoDB data through a NAT gateway, and NAT data processing charges are high. What is the most cost-effective change?",["Replace the NAT gateway with a self-managed NAT instance on a larger instance type","Add a DynamoDB gateway VPC endpoint to the private route tables","Add a second NAT gateway in each Availability Zone","Turn on DynamoDB Accelerator in front of the table"],1,"Gateway endpoints for DynamoDB and S3 have no hourly or data processing charge and take that traffic off the NAT gateway. A NAT instance still processes the traffic and must be managed, more NAT gateways add cost, and DAX does not change the network path for writes.","4.4 Cost-optimized network architectures",["A NAT instance still processes all the traffic, adds management work, and can become a bottleneck or single point of failure.",null,"More NAT gateways add hourly charges and still charge per GB processed for the DynamoDB traffic.","DAX caches reads in the VPC, but writes and cache misses still reach DynamoDB through the NAT gateway."],2],["saa87",0,4,"Which type of data transfer is generally free of charge on AWS?",["Data transferred in to AWS from the internet","Data transferred out from EC2 to the internet","Data transferred between two AWS Regions","Data sent between AZs over private IP addresses"],0,"Inbound data transfer from the internet is free. Data out to the internet, between Regions and between Availability Zones is charged per GB, which is why architects keep chatty traffic in one AZ where it is safe and use CloudFront to reduce egress.","4.4 Cost-optimized network architectures",[null,"Data out from EC2 to the internet is charged per GB and is often the largest transfer cost.","Transfer between Regions is charged per GB, for example for cross-Region replication.","Traffic between Availability Zones is charged per GB in each direction, even over private IP addresses."],1],["saa88",0,4,"The finance team wants an email as soon as the forecasted monthly AWS spend for a project goes above $10,000, before the month ends. What should the architect set up?",["A monthly Cost Explorer report that is exported and emailed to the finance team","A CloudWatch alarm on each EC2 instance's CPU utilization","A Cost and Usage Report that is delivered to an S3 bucket","An AWS Budgets cost budget with an alert on forecasted spend"],3,"AWS Budgets can alert on actual or forecasted cost and filter by tags or accounts. Cost Explorer is for analysis rather than alerts, CPU alarms do not measure spend, and the Cost and Usage Report is detailed billing data with no alerting of its own.","Domain 4: cost management tools (AWS Budgets)",["A monthly report arrives after the month is over, so it cannot warn the team when the forecast first exceeds the limit.","CPU utilization alarms measure performance, not spending, so they cannot track a cost threshold.","The Cost and Usage Report is detailed billing data delivered to S3; it has no built-in alerting on forecasted spend.",null],3],["saa89",0,4,"Several projects share the same AWS accounts, and finance needs monthly costs broken down by project. What should the architect do?",["Tag resources with a project key and activate it as a cost allocation tag","Create a separate VPC for each project in the same account","Enable detailed CloudWatch monitoring on all the resources","Turn on AWS CloudTrail data events for every service"],0,"Once a user-defined tag is activated as a cost allocation tag, Cost Explorer and the Cost and Usage Report can group costs by it; tag policies help keep tagging consistent. VPCs do not split billing, and CloudWatch and CloudTrail track metrics and API calls, not cost.","Domain 4: cost allocation tags",[null,"VPCs are network boundaries; costs are not split by VPC, and many costs such as S3 are not tied to a VPC at all.","Detailed monitoring gives more frequent performance metrics and adds cost; it does not attribute spend to projects.","CloudTrail data events log API calls for auditing; they do not break down billing by project."],1],["saa90",0,4,"A company suspects many EC2 instances are over-provisioned. It wants recommendations for smaller or different instance types based on actual CPU and memory usage. Which service provides this?",["AWS Trusted Advisor security checks","AWS Compute Optimizer","Amazon Inspector","AWS Config conformance packs"],1,"Compute Optimizer analyzes CloudWatch metrics with machine learning and recommends right-sized EC2 instances, Auto Scaling groups, EBS volumes and Lambda memory. Trusted Advisor security checks, Inspector and Config do not size instances.","4.2 Cost-optimized compute solutions",["Trusted Advisor security checks look at issues such as open ports and MFA, not instance sizing based on usage.",null,"Inspector scans for software vulnerabilities and network exposure; it does not recommend instance sizes.","Config conformance packs evaluate configuration compliance against rules; they do not analyze utilization to right-size instances."],1],["x1",0,1,"In a single AWS account, an IAM user has no identity-based policy that mentions a particular S3 bucket. That bucket's policy grants the user's ARN s3:GetObject, and no policy anywhere contains a deny. What happens when the user downloads an object?",["It is denied, because the user's own identity-based policy must also contain an allow for s3:GetObject","It is denied unless the user belongs to an IAM group with an S3 policy","It is allowed, because in one account an allow in either policy type is enough","It is allowed only when the request is sent through an S3 access point"],2,"Within the same account, IAM evaluates identity-based and resource-based policies together, and an allow in either one grants access when there is no explicit deny. The need for an allow on both sides only applies to cross-account requests.","Domain 1: IAM policy evaluation logic",["Needing an allow on both sides applies to cross-account requests; within one account the bucket policy alone can grant access.","Group membership is just another identity-based source; it is not required when the bucket policy already allows the user.",null,"Access points are optional; a plain bucket request is evaluated against the bucket policy in the normal way."],3],["x2",0,1,"An IAM role in account B has an identity policy that allows s3:GetObject on a bucket owned by account A. The bucket uses SSE-S3 encryption, yet the role gets AccessDenied. What is most likely missing?",["A bucket policy in account A granting the account B role access","A service control policy in account B that explicitly allows Amazon S3 actions","A KMS grant that lets the role use the key protecting the objects","An S3 gateway endpoint policy in account A that names the role"],0,"For cross-account access, both sides must allow the request: the caller's identity policy in account B and a resource-based policy (the bucket policy) in account A. SSE-S3 keys are managed by S3, so no KMS permission is involved, which rules out the KMS grant.","Domain 1: Cross-account resource-based policies",[null,"SCPs never grant permissions, and the default FullAWSAccess SCP already permits S3, so an SCP is not what is missing.","SSE-S3 keys are managed entirely by S3, so no KMS key permission or grant is involved in reading these objects.","Endpoint policies only matter for traffic through that endpoint in the caller's VPC; they cannot replace the bucket owner's resource policy."],3],["x3",0,1,"A web application needs sign-up and sign-in pages, optional MFA and a managed user directory. Its Amazon API Gateway REST API must accept only requests that carry a valid token from a signed-in user. What should the architect use?",["A Cognito identity pool alone, with the API set to IAM authorization for guests","A Cognito user pool, with a Cognito user pool authorizer on the API","An IAM user for each customer, with access keys handed out at sign-up","STS GetFederationToken calls made by the browser for every visitor"],1,"A Cognito user pool is the user directory that handles sign-up, sign-in and MFA and issues JWTs, and API Gateway can validate those tokens with a user pool authorizer. An identity pool only exchanges identities for temporary AWS credentials; it is not a user directory.","Domain 1: Cognito user pools vs identity pools",["An identity pool hands out AWS credentials but has no user directory, sign-up pages or MFA of its own.",null,"Creating IAM users and long-term access keys for customers breaks least privilege and does not scale to public users.","GetFederationToken needs long-term IAM credentials to call, so browsers cannot safely use it, and it offers no sign-in."],2],["x4",0,1,"EC2 instances in private subnets with no NAT gateway must fetch secrets from AWS Secrets Manager. Traffic must stay on the AWS network. What should the architect add?",["A gateway VPC endpoint for Secrets Manager in the private route tables","A NAT instance in a public subnet with a route from the private subnets","A VPC peering connection to the VPC that hosts Secrets Manager","An interface VPC endpoint for Secrets Manager with private DNS enabled"],3,"Secrets Manager is reached privately through an interface endpoint (AWS PrivateLink), which places ENIs in the subnets; with private DNS the normal service hostname resolves to them. Gateway endpoints exist only for S3 and DynamoDB, so a gateway endpoint for Secrets Manager is not possible.","Domain 1: Interface endpoints (PrivateLink)",["Gateway endpoints exist only for S3 and DynamoDB; Secrets Manager is reached through an interface endpoint.","A NAT instance sends the traffic out to the public service endpoint, which is what the design is trying to avoid.","AWS services are not reached through peering into a service-owned VPC; PrivateLink interface endpoints are the mechanism.",null],2],["x5",0,1,"Instances in private subnets reach Amazon S3 through a gateway VPC endpoint. Security wants to stop anyone copying data from those instances to S3 buckets outside the company. What control does this?",["An endpoint policy allowing access only to the company's buckets","Bucket policies on the company's buckets that require the aws:SourceVpce condition key","A security group attached to the gateway endpoint that allows only company bucket names","A network ACL rule on the private subnets that lists the approved bucket names"],0,"A VPC endpoint policy controls which resources can be reached through the endpoint, so limiting it to company buckets blocks uploads to outside buckets. Bucket policies with aws:SourceVpce restrict who can reach the company's own buckets, but do nothing to stop writes to a bucket someone else owns.","Domain 1: VPC endpoint policies",[null,"aws:SourceVpce only restricts access to the company's own buckets; it cannot stop uploads to a bucket owned by someone else.","Gateway endpoints are route table targets and do not support security groups, and security groups cannot filter bucket names.","Network ACLs filter by IP address, port and protocol only; they have no way to match S3 bucket names."],3],["x6",0,1,"A small company serves its website through Amazon CloudFront with DNS in Route 53 and has never bought any extra security service. What protection does it have against common network and transport layer DDoS attacks?",["None, until it subscribes to AWS Shield Advanced","AWS Shield Standard, applied automatically at no extra charge","AWS WAF managed rules, which are enabled on every distribution","Amazon GuardDuty, which blocks attack traffic at the edge"],1,"AWS Shield Standard is included automatically for all AWS customers and defends against common layer 3 and 4 attacks, notably on CloudFront and Route 53. Shield Advanced adds extras such as the response team and cost protection, but basic protection does not depend on it.","Domain 1: Shield Standard vs Shield Advanced",["Basic DDoS protection does not wait for Shield Advanced; Shield Standard is always on for every customer.",null,"AWS WAF is a separate, paid service that you must configure with a web ACL; it is not turned on by default.","GuardDuty detects threats by analyzing logs; it does not sit in the traffic path or block DDoS traffic."],1],["x7",0,1,"A company runs its application in eu-west-1 and puts an Amazon CloudFront distribution in front of it with a custom domain name. The team imported a certificate into ACM in eu-west-1, but it does not appear when they edit the distribution. What is the cause?",["CloudFront accepts only private certificates issued by AWS Private CA","The certificate must be validated by email instead of by DNS records","CloudFront only uses ACM certificates from US East (N. Virginia)","CloudFront distributions can only use certificates stored in IAM"],2,"CloudFront is a global service that reads ACM certificates from us-east-1 only, so the certificate must be requested or imported there. The validation method (DNS or email) does not affect which Region's certificates CloudFront can list.","Domain 1: ACM certificates for CloudFront",["CloudFront works with public ACM certificates; there is no rule that limits it to Private CA certificates.","The validation method has nothing to do with which Region's certificates CloudFront can see.",null,"CloudFront can use certificates in the IAM certificate store, but ACM certificates in us-east-1 are also supported and preferred."],1],["x8",0,1,"A bucket in account A encrypts objects with SSE-KMS using a customer managed key in account A. A role in account B is already allowed by the bucket policy, but GetObject fails with a KMS access error. What else is required?",["Copy the key material into account B and create a matching key there","Switch the bucket to the AWS managed key aws/s3 so both accounts can use it","Attach an SCP to account B that allows kms:Decrypt on all keys","Allow account B in the key policy and give the role kms:Decrypt on that key"],3,"Cross-account use of a KMS key needs both the key policy in the owning account to allow the other account and an IAM policy in account B that grants the role kms:Decrypt on the key. AWS managed keys cannot have their key policies edited, so they cannot be shared across accounts.","Domain 1: KMS key policies and cross-account use",["KMS key material cannot be exported, and a different key in account B could not decrypt data encrypted under account A's key.","AWS managed keys have key policies you cannot edit, so they cannot be opened to another account.","SCPs never grant permissions, and the key policy in account A would still not trust account B.",null],3],["x9",0,1,"A company must generate and hold its own encryption keys outside AWS. It is willing to send the key with each request so S3 can encrypt and decrypt objects, but AWS must not store the key. Which option fits?",["SSE-C, server-side encryption with customer-provided keys","SSE-KMS using a KMS key with imported key material","SSE-S3, server-side encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys","SSE-KMS using the AWS managed key for Amazon S3"],0,"With SSE-C the client supplies the key on every request, S3 uses it to encrypt or decrypt and then discards it, keeping only a value to verify later requests. Imported key material is still stored and used inside AWS KMS, so it does not meet the 'AWS must not store the key' rule.","Domain 1: S3 encryption options (SSE-C)",[null,"Imported key material still lives inside AWS KMS and is used there, so AWS would be storing the key.","SSE-S3 keys are created and stored by S3, which breaks the rule that the company holds its own keys.","The AWS managed key is created and fully controlled by AWS, the opposite of what the company requires."],1],["x10",0,1,"A company wants a guarantee that no S3 bucket in an account can become public, even if a developer later writes a bucket policy or ACL that grants public access. What should the architect enable?",["An AWS Config rule that reports buckets allowing public read access","S3 Block Public Access settings at the account level","Amazon Macie scans of every bucket in the account each day","Default encryption with SSE-KMS on every bucket in the account"],1,"Account-level S3 Block Public Access overrides bucket policies and ACLs that would grant public access, so it prevents exposure rather than detecting it. An AWS Config rule only reports a public bucket after the fact and does not stop the change.","Domain 1: S3 Block Public Access",["A Config rule detects a public bucket after the change is made; it does not prevent the policy from taking effect.",null,"Macie finds sensitive data and reports on bucket exposure, but it does not block public access settings.","Encryption at rest does not stop public reads, because S3 decrypts objects for any authorized request, including anonymous ones."],2],["x11",0,1,"A versioned S3 bucket holds financial records. The company wants anyone who permanently deletes an object version or suspends versioning to have to supply an MFA code. How is this set up?",["Enable Object Lock in governance mode on the bucket","Rely on versioning, which requires MFA for version deletes by default","Enable MFA Delete with the root user's credentials via API or CLI","Enable MFA Delete in the S3 console as any user with full S3 permissions"],2,"MFA Delete requires an MFA code to permanently delete versions or change the versioning state, and it can only be enabled by the bucket owner's root user through the API or CLI. The console option and non-root administrators cannot turn it on, which is why the fourth option fails.","Domain 1: S3 versioning and MFA Delete",["Governance-mode Object Lock protects retained versions but does not require an MFA code for deletes or versioning changes.","Versioning alone keeps old versions but lets authorized users delete them without any MFA code.",null,"MFA Delete cannot be enabled in the console, and only the root user of the bucket owner can turn it on."],3],["x12",0,1,"A company wants continuous, automated scanning of its EC2 instances and Amazon ECR container images for known software vulnerabilities, plus reports of unintended network reachability. Which service provides this?",["Amazon GuardDuty","Amazon Macie","AWS Trusted Advisor","Amazon Inspector"],3,"Amazon Inspector scans EC2 instances, ECR images and Lambda functions for known CVEs and assesses network reachability of instances. GuardDuty detects threats from log analysis, such as malicious traffic, but it does not inventory software vulnerabilities.","Domain 1: Amazon Inspector",["GuardDuty detects threats from logs and events; it does not scan software packages for known vulnerabilities.","Macie discovers sensitive data in S3; it does not look at instances or container images at all.","Trusted Advisor checks account configuration against best practices but does not scan workloads for CVEs.",null],1],["x13",0,1,"A company with 50 accounts wants one place to view GuardDuty, Inspector and Macie findings from every account and to run automated checks against the AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standard. What should the architect set up?",["AWS Security Hub with a delegated administrator account for the organization","An AWS Config aggregator in the security account","Amazon Detective enabled in the management account","A CloudTrail Lake event data store for all accounts"],0,"Security Hub aggregates findings from GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie and other services across an organization and runs security standards checks. A Config aggregator collects configuration and compliance data only; it does not gather findings from those other services.","Domain 1: AWS Security Hub",[null,"A Config aggregator collects configuration and compliance data only, not GuardDuty, Inspector or Macie findings.","Detective helps investigate the root cause of findings; it is not the central findings and standards-checking service.","CloudTrail Lake stores and queries API activity, not security findings or best-practice checks."],1],["x14",0,1,"An organization must log API activity from every current and future member account into one S3 bucket in a log archive account, and member account administrators must not be able to change or delete that logging. What should the architect create?",["A separate trail in each account, created once by an administrator","An organization trail created from the management account","An AWS Config aggregator that collects every account's resource history","VPC Flow Logs in each VPC, published to the log archive account"],1,"An organization trail logs events for all member accounts, including ones added later, and member accounts can see it but cannot modify or delete it. Per-account trails can be stopped by each account's administrators and do not cover new accounts automatically.","Domain 1: CloudTrail organization trails",["Per-account trails can be stopped by each account's administrators and are not created automatically for new accounts.",null,"AWS Config records resource configuration changes, not a full log of API activity across accounts.","VPC Flow Logs capture network traffic metadata, not the API calls that CloudTrail records."],2],["x15",0,1,"An IAM role's identity policy allows s3:* and ec2:*. The role has a permissions boundary that allows s3:* and dynamodb:*. No other policies apply. Which actions can the role perform?",["S3, EC2 and DynamoDB actions, the union of both policies","S3 and EC2 actions, because the identity policy grants the permissions","Only S3 actions, where the identity policy and the boundary overlap","No actions, because a permissions boundary acts as an explicit deny"],2,"A permissions boundary sets the maximum permissions; effective permissions are the intersection of the identity policy and the boundary, which here is only S3. The boundary grants nothing by itself, so DynamoDB is not allowed even though the boundary lists it.","Domain 1: Permissions boundaries",["Boundaries do not add permissions; the result is the overlap of the two policies, not their union.","The boundary caps what the identity policy can grant, so EC2 actions outside the boundary are not allowed.",null,"A boundary is a limit, not a deny; actions allowed by both policies, here S3, are still permitted."],3],["x16",0,1,"Application servers listen on port 8080 in private subnets behind an Application Load Balancer. They must accept traffic only from the load balancer, whose IP addresses change over time. How should the architect configure access?",["Allow the ALB subnets' CIDR ranges on port 8080 in the network ACL only","Allow the current private IP addresses of the ALB's network interfaces","Allow 0.0.0.0/0 on port 8080 and rely on the subnets being private","Allow port 8080 in the app security group with the ALB's security group as source"],3,"Referencing the ALB's security group as the source allows traffic from any interface in that group, so it keeps working as the load balancer's IPs change. Hard-coding the ALB's current IPs breaks when AWS replaces or scales the load balancer nodes.","Domain 1: Security group referencing",["Subnet CIDR rules in a NACL also admit any other resource in those subnets and leave the security group open.","ALB node IP addresses change as the load balancer scales or is replaced, so fixed IP rules break over time.","Private subnets block direct internet traffic, but 0.0.0.0/0 still admits every resource in the VPC and any peered network.",null],2],["x17",0,1,"An application needs dozens of configuration values, a few of which are sensitive and must be encrypted. None need automatic rotation, and the company wants the lowest-cost managed store. What should the architect use?",["Systems Manager Parameter Store standard parameters, with SecureString for sensitive values","AWS Secrets Manager for every value, including non-sensitive settings","A JSON file in S3 encrypted with SSE-S3 and downloaded at startup","Plain-text Lambda environment variables set in the function configuration"],0,"Parameter Store standard parameters carry no additional charge and SecureString encrypts sensitive values with KMS, which fits when rotation is not needed. Secrets Manager adds managed rotation but charges per secret, which is unnecessary cost for plain configuration.","Domain 1: Parameter Store vs Secrets Manager",[null,"Secrets Manager charges per secret and adds rotation the application does not need, so it costs more for plain settings.","A self-managed S3 file needs custom loading code and gives no per-value access control or change history.","Plain-text environment variables leave sensitive values unencrypted in the function configuration."],2],["x18",0,1,"A company in AWS Organizations must block use of every Region except eu-west-1 and eu-central-1 in all accounts, while still letting global services such as IAM work. What is the most effective control?",["Disable all other Regions for each account in the account settings page","An SCP that denies actions when aws:RequestedRegion is not an approved Region, exempting global services","An AWS Config rule in each account that flags resources in other Regions","An IAM policy in each account that denies ec2:* outside the two Regions"],1,"An SCP with a Deny on aws:RequestedRegion applies to every principal in the member accounts, and excluding global services keeps IAM and similar services working. Default Regions cannot be switched off in account settings; only opt-in Regions can be disabled, so the first option cannot work.","Domain 1: SCP Region restriction",["Only opt-in Regions can be disabled; default Regions stay enabled, so this setting cannot block them.",null,"A Config rule only reports resources after they are created; it does not prevent the API calls.","Per-account IAM policies can be edited by account administrators, and ec2:* alone leaves every other service usable."],3],["x19",0,2,"A financial trading service uses a custom TCP protocol, must handle millions of requests per second with very low latency, needs one static IP address per Availability Zone, and must see the client's source IP. Which load balancer fits?",["Application Load Balancer","Classic Load Balancer","Network Load Balancer","Gateway Load Balancer"],2,"A Network Load Balancer works at layer 4, handles very high request rates at low latency, supports a static or Elastic IP per AZ and can preserve client source IPs. An Application Load Balancer handles HTTP and HTTPS at layer 7 and does not offer static IP addresses.","Domain 2: ALB vs NLB",["An ALB handles HTTP and HTTPS at layer 7 and has no static IP addresses, so it cannot serve a custom TCP protocol this way.","The Classic Load Balancer is a previous-generation service without static IPs per AZ or the NLB's performance.",null,"A Gateway Load Balancer inserts third-party virtual appliances such as firewalls into the traffic path; it does not front applications."],1],["x20",0,2,"Workers poll an SQS standard queue, and each message takes about two minutes to process. The queue uses the default visibility timeout, and many orders are being processed twice. What should the architect change?",["Turn on long polling by setting a receive wait time","Switch to a FIFO queue to get exactly-once processing","Shorten the message retention period of the queue","Increase the queue's visibility timeout"],3,"The default visibility timeout is 30 seconds, so messages reappear while still being worked on and another worker picks them up. Setting it above the processing time fixes this. Long polling only reduces empty receives and does not stop messages becoming visible again.","Domain 2: SQS visibility timeout",["Long polling reduces empty receives but does not stop messages becoming visible again while still being processed.","Moving to FIFO does not fix a timeout shorter than processing time, and it changes throughput and ordering behavior.","A shorter retention period makes messages expire sooner; it does nothing about duplicate delivery during processing.",null],3],["x21",0,2,"A company wants a Lambda function to run whenever any EC2 instance changes state, and it also wants to receive events from a supported SaaS partner application in the same event-routing service. What should the architect use?",["EventBridge rules that match the events and invoke the function","An SNS topic that EC2 publishes instance state changes to directly","CloudTrail Insights with a Lambda function subscribed to it","An SQS queue that the function polls for EC2 state changes"],0,"EventBridge receives AWS service events such as EC2 state-change notifications and also SaaS partner events, and rules route matching events to targets like Lambda. EC2 does not publish state changes to an SNS topic on its own; that would still need an EventBridge rule.","Domain 2: EventBridge event-driven design",[null,"EC2 does not publish state changes straight to SNS; routing those events is EventBridge's job.","CloudTrail Insights spots unusual API call volumes; it is not an event bus for state changes or SaaS events.","EC2 does not write state changes to SQS on its own, and polling adds delay and code without routing rules."],1],["x22",0,2,"A serverless REST API built on API Gateway and Lambda is offered to external partners. One partner's bursts must not be able to overwhelm the back end for the others. What should the architect configure?",["Reserved concurrency on the Lambda function behind the API","API Gateway usage plans with API keys and throttling","A CloudFront distribution in front of the API to cache responses","An AWS WAF geographic match rule for each partner's country"],1,"Usage plans attach throttling and quota limits to each API key, so each partner is limited separately. Reserved concurrency caps the whole function, which protects the back end but lets one partner use up the shared capacity for everyone else.","Domain 2: API Gateway throttling and usage plans",["Reserved concurrency caps the function as a whole, so one partner's burst can still use up the shared limit.",null,"Caching helps only repeated GET responses and does not limit how many requests a single partner can send.","Geographic rules block or allow countries; they do not apply fair per-client request limits."],2],["x23",0,2,"A company runs its applications on Kubernetes on premises, using Helm charts and kubectl-based tooling. It wants a managed control plane on AWS with minimal changes to its deployment process. What should the architect recommend?",["Amazon ECS with the EC2 launch type","AWS Elastic Beanstalk with Docker platforms","Amazon EKS","AWS App Runner"],2,"Amazon EKS runs a managed, conformant Kubernetes control plane, so existing Helm charts and kubectl tooling keep working. ECS is a container orchestrator with its own task definitions and API, so moving to it would mean rewriting deployments.","Domain 2: ECS vs EKS",["ECS uses its own task definitions and API, so the Helm charts and kubectl tooling would have to be replaced.","Elastic Beanstalk manages application environments, not a Kubernetes API that Helm and kubectl can target.",null,"App Runner runs web services from code or images without exposing Kubernetes, so the existing tooling would not carry over."],1],["x24",0,2,"An Aurora MySQL cluster has a single writer instance. The company wants automatic failover inside the Region if the writer fails and wants to spread read traffic. What should the architect do?",["Create a cross-Region read replica and promote it when the writer fails","Place Amazon RDS Proxy in front of the single writer instance","Enable the RDS Multi-AZ standby option used by RDS for MySQL","Add Aurora Replicas in other AZs; use cluster and reader endpoints"],3,"Aurora Replicas share the cluster volume, serve reads through the reader endpoint and are promoted automatically if the writer fails, with the cluster endpoint following the new writer. A cross-Region replica needs manual promotion and is aimed at Regional DR, not fast in-Region failover.","Domain 2: Aurora replicas and endpoints",["A cross-Region replica needs manual promotion and is aimed at Regional disaster recovery, not fast in-Region failover.","RDS Proxy pools connections and speeds failover handling, but it does not create a replica to fail over to.","Aurora does not use the RDS Multi-AZ standby model; its Aurora Replicas are the failover targets.",null],2],["x25",0,2,"An application runs in us-east-1, eu-west-1 and ap-southeast-1. Each user should be sent to whichever Region gives them the fastest response. Which Route 53 routing policy fits?",["Latency-based routing","Geolocation routing","Multivalue answer routing","Weighted routing"],0,"Latency-based routing answers with the record for the Region that has the lowest measured latency for the user's network location. Geolocation routes by where the user is, which is not always the fastest Region.","Domain 2: Route 53 routing policies",[null,"Geolocation routes by the user's location, which does not always match the Region with the lowest latency.","Multivalue answer returns several healthy records at random; it does not consider latency.","Weighted routing splits traffic by fixed proportions, not by which Region is fastest for each user."],1],["x26",0,2,"For legal reasons, users in Germany must be served by a stack in eu-central-1, and everyone else by a stack in us-east-1. Which Route 53 configuration meets this?",["Latency-based routing records for the two Regions","Geolocation routing with Germany and default records","Weighted routing with most weight on the us-east-1 record","Failover routing with eu-central-1 as the primary record"],1,"Geolocation routing answers based on the user's location, so a Germany record plus a default record for everyone else enforces the rule. Latency-based routing could send German users to us-east-1 whenever that is measured as faster, which breaks the legal requirement.","Domain 2: Route 53 geolocation routing",["Latency-based routing can send German users to us-east-1 whenever it measures faster, breaking the legal rule.",null,"Weighted routing splits traffic by percentage and ignores where the user is located.","Failover routing sends everyone to the primary while it is healthy, so all users would reach eu-central-1."],2],["x27",0,2,"A company has six web servers without a load balancer. It wants DNS to return several server IP addresses to clients and to stop returning any server that fails a health check. Which Route 53 routing policy fits?",["Simple routing with all six IP addresses in one record","Failover routing with one primary and five secondary records","Multivalue answer routing with a health check on each record","Geoproximity routing with a bias on each server"],2,"Multivalue answer routing returns up to eight healthy records chosen at random and leaves out records whose health checks fail. Simple routing can hold several values but cannot attach health checks, so it keeps returning failed servers.","Domain 2: Route 53 multivalue answer routing",["Simple routing can return several values, but it cannot attach health checks, so failed servers keep being returned.","Failover routing supports one primary and one secondary record; it is not built to share load across six servers.",null,"Geoproximity routes by distance between users and resources with a bias, not to spread load across healthy servers."],2],["x28",0,2,"A company runs physical and VMware servers on premises. It wants continuous block-level replication to AWS with an RPO measured in seconds, a low-cost staging area, and the ability to launch recovery instances within minutes. What should the architect use?",["AWS Backup with cross-Region copies of on-premises backups","AWS DataSync tasks scheduled every few minutes","Amazon S3 Cross-Region Replication of exported disk images","AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery"],3,"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery continuously replicates source servers at block level into a lightweight staging area and launches full recovery instances on demand. DataSync copies files on a schedule, so it cannot give second-level RPO or launch servers.","Domain 2: AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery",["AWS Backup takes periodic backups, so recovery point objectives are hours rather than seconds, with slow server rebuilds.","DataSync copies files on a schedule; it does not replicate block devices or launch recovery servers.","Exported disk images copied between buckets give neither continuous replication nor quick server launches.",null],3],["x29",0,2,"A pilot light environment in us-west-2 fails to launch its application tier during a DR test because the launch template references a custom AMI ID that exists only in us-east-1. What should the architect do?",["Copy the AMIs to us-west-2 and use the new AMI IDs there","Share the AMI with the account so it becomes visible in every Region","Make the AMI public so that every Region can launch from it","Enable EBS snapshot replication, which copies AMIs to all Regions"],0,"AMIs are Regional resources, so they must be copied to the recovery Region, where they get new IDs that the templates must use. Sharing an AMI grants other accounts access in the same Region; it does not make the AMI available in another Region.","Domain 2: AMI copies for DR",[null,"Sharing an AMI grants other accounts access in the same Region; it does not make the AMI exist in another Region.","Making an AMI public exposes it to everyone but still leaves it in its original Region only.","EBS snapshots are not replicated to other Regions automatically; AMIs and snapshots must be copied explicitly."],2],["x30",0,2,"A security team must deploy the same IAM roles and AWS Config rules to every account in an organizational unit, including accounts added to the OU later. What should the architect use?",["CloudFormation nested stacks deployed from the management account","CloudFormation StackSets with service-managed permissions and automatic deployment","CloudFormation change sets reviewed before each deployment","A separate stack created by hand in each account"],1,"StackSets with service-managed permissions deploy stacks across accounts in an OU, and automatic deployment adds the stack to accounts that join the OU later. Nested stacks only compose templates within one stack in one account and Region.","Domain 2: CloudFormation StackSets",["Nested stacks reuse templates inside one stack in one account and Region; they do not deploy across accounts.",null,"Change sets preview updates to a single stack; they do not spread stacks to many accounts or future ones.","Manual stacks in each account take ongoing effort and miss accounts that join the OU later."],2],["x31",0,2,"During a DR test, an Auto Scaling group in the recovery Region cannot launch enough instances and reports that a vCPU limit was exceeded. The same group runs fine in the primary Region. What should the architect do?",["Raise the quota in the primary Region, since quotas apply to all Regions","Use Spot Instances in the recovery Region, which are not counted in quotas","Request vCPU quota increases in the recovery Region in advance","Wait for Trusted Advisor to raise the limit automatically during failover"],2,"Most service quotas, including EC2 vCPU limits, are set per account per Region, so the recovery Region needs its own increases requested in advance. Raising the quota in the primary Region does nothing for the recovery Region.","Domain 2: Service quotas and DR planning",["EC2 vCPU quotas are set per Region, so raising the primary Region's quota does not help the recovery Region.","Spot Instances have their own vCPU quotas and can also be unavailable during a large Regional event.",null,"Trusted Advisor can show quota usage but does not raise quotas; increases must be requested."],3],["x32",0,2,"A burst of events causes one Lambda function to use nearly all of the account's concurrency, and a critical payment function in the same Region starts being throttled. What should the architect do to protect the payment function?",["Increase the memory setting of the payment function","Configure provisioned concurrency on the busy function","Move the busy function into a different AWS account","Configure reserved concurrency on the payment function"],3,"Reserved concurrency sets aside part of the account's concurrency for one function, so other functions cannot consume it. Provisioned concurrency on the busy function pre-initializes environments for speed but does not stop it from taking the rest of the pool.","Domain 2: Lambda concurrency and throttling",["More memory speeds up each invocation but does not reserve concurrency, so the function can still be throttled.","Provisioned concurrency pre-warms environments on the busy function but does not stop it using the rest of the pool.","Moving the busy function to another account adds operational work when reserved concurrency solves it directly.",null],3],["x33",0,2,"An application needs at least six running instances to handle peak load and must keep full capacity if any single Availability Zone fails. Which Auto Scaling group layout meets this at the lowest cost?",["Nine instances, three in each of three AZs","Six instances spread three per AZ across two AZs","Six instances spread two per AZ across three AZs","Twelve instances spread six per AZ across two AZs"],0,"With three per AZ in three AZs, losing one AZ still leaves six instances, using nine in total. Twelve across two AZs also survives an AZ loss but costs three more instances, and both six-instance layouts drop below six when an AZ fails.","Domain 2: Multi-AZ capacity planning",[null,"Losing one of two AZs leaves only three instances, half the capacity the application needs.","Losing one AZ leaves only four instances, below the minimum of six.","Twelve across two AZs survives an AZ loss but uses three more instances than the three-AZ layout."],3],["x34",0,2,"A company must keep monthly backups of a DynamoDB table for seven years to meet an audit requirement. What should the architect use?",["Point-in-time recovery with its retention set to seven years","On-demand backups scheduled by an AWS Backup plan with seven-year retention","DynamoDB global tables with a replica in a second Region","DynamoDB Streams with a Lambda function that deletes old items"],1,"On-demand backups are kept until deleted, and an AWS Backup plan can take them monthly and apply a long retention rule. Point-in-time recovery only lets you restore to a point within at most the last 35 days, so it cannot hold seven years of history.","Domain 2: DynamoDB backups",["Point-in-time recovery covers at most the last 35 days, so it cannot keep seven years of monthly restore points.",null,"Global tables replicate current data between Regions, so an accidental change or deletion is replicated too; they are not backups.","Streams capture item changes for 24 hours; deleting old items is the opposite of keeping long-term backups."],2],["x35",0,3,"A company runs a large Cassandra cluster of dozens of nodes on EC2 and wants replicas of each data set placed on separate racks, so one rack failure affects only some nodes. The database should also know which rack each node is on. Which placement strategy fits?",["A cluster placement group in one Availability Zone","A spread placement group across the Availability Zones","A partition placement group, with replicas in different partitions","Dedicated Hosts, with one host allocated for each node"],2,"Partition placement groups put groups of instances on separate racks and expose the partition number, so topology-aware systems like Cassandra, HDFS and Kafka can place replicas in different partitions. Spread groups isolate each instance but allow only seven running instances per AZ, too few for a large cluster.","Domain 3: Placement groups",["A cluster group packs instances close together, often on shared hardware, so one rack failure can take out many nodes.","Spread groups allow only seven running instances per AZ, too few for a large cluster of dozens of nodes.",null,"Dedicated Hosts give per-host isolation for licensing but do not expose rack placement to the database."],3],["x36",0,3,"A tightly coupled MPI weather model needs inter-node communication that bypasses the operating system network stack to get the lowest latency possible on EC2. What should the architect attach to the instances?",["An additional elastic network interface on each instance","An Elastic IP address on each instance in the cluster","Enhanced networking with ENA only, the default on current types","An Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA)"],3,"EFA adds OS-bypass capability for HPC and machine learning workloads using MPI or NCCL, cutting latency between instances. ENA provides enhanced networking with high bandwidth, but traffic still goes through the kernel network stack.","Domain 3: Enhanced networking with ENA and EFA",["More ENIs add network paths but still use the kernel network stack, so they do not give OS bypass.","Elastic IP addresses give public, static addresses; they do nothing for latency between cluster nodes.","ENA gives high bandwidth but traffic still goes through the kernel, so it cannot match EFA's OS-bypass latency.",null],2],["x37",0,3,"A video transcoding job needs very fast temporary scratch space for intermediate files. The files are copies of source data kept in S3, so losing them when an instance stops is acceptable. Which storage gives the highest I/O at no extra volume cost?",["Instance store volumes on an instance type that includes them","A Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2) EBS volume attached to the instance","An Amazon EFS file system in Max I/O mode shared by the instances","A General Purpose SSD (gp3) EBS volume with its IOPS raised"],0,"Instance store disks are physically attached to the host, give very high I/O and are included in the instance price, but their data is lost when the instance stops or terminates, which is acceptable here. An io2 volume is durable and fast, but it is billed separately for capacity and IOPS.","Domain 3: Instance store",[null,"io2 is durable and fast, but it is billed separately for storage and IOPS, which the workload does not need.","EFS is a network file system with higher latency than local disks and adds storage charges.","gp3 is network-attached and billed separately, and even with higher IOPS it cannot match local instance store I/O."],2],["x38",0,3,"A company is moving a NAS that serves the same data to Linux clients over NFS and to Windows clients over SMB, and it relies on NetApp snapshots and SnapMirror replication. Which managed service is the best fit?",["Amazon EFS with lifecycle management enabled","Amazon FSx for NetApp ONTAP","Amazon FSx for Windows File Server","Amazon FSx for Lustre linked to S3"],1,"FSx for NetApp ONTAP supports NFS and SMB access to the same data and provides ONTAP features such as snapshots and SnapMirror. FSx for Windows File Server serves SMB for Windows workloads but not ONTAP replication or multiprotocol NFS use of the same volumes.","Domain 3: Shared file systems (FSx for NetApp ONTAP)",["EFS serves NFS only and has no SMB access or ONTAP features such as SnapMirror.",null,"FSx for Windows File Server serves SMB and has no ONTAP SnapMirror or multiprotocol NFS use of the same volumes.","FSx for Lustre is a high-performance file system for compute clusters, not a multiprotocol NAS replacement."],1],["x39",0,3,"An analytics application downloads multi-gigabyte objects from S3 in the same Region using a single request per object, and each download is slow. How can the architect speed up the downloads?",["Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket","Turn on S3 Versioning for the bucket","Use parallel byte-range fetches","Upload the objects again using multipart upload"],2,"Byte-range fetches let clients download parts of an object over many parallel connections, which raises throughput for large objects. Transfer Acceleration speeds long-distance transfers through edge locations, which does little for a client in the same Region.","Domain 3: S3 byte-range fetches",["Transfer Acceleration helps long-distance transfers through edge locations; the client is already in the same Region.","Versioning keeps previous object versions and has no effect on download speed.",null,"Multipart upload speeds uploads; downloading each object with one request stays as slow as before."],2],["x40",0,3,"A game needs an in-memory cache that keeps sorted leaderboards, replicates data to a replica for failover, and can persist data to disk. Which service fits best?",["Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached","DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","Amazon CloudFront with a long TTL","Amazon ElastiCache for Redis"],3,"ElastiCache for Redis offers data structures such as sorted sets, replication with automatic failover, and persistence options. Memcached is a simple, multithreaded key-value cache with no replication or persistence, so it cannot meet those needs.","Domain 3: ElastiCache for Redis vs Memcached",["Memcached has no replication, persistence or sorted set data type, so it cannot meet any of the three needs.","DAX caches DynamoDB reads only; it is not a general in-memory store with sorted sets.","CloudFront caches HTTP content at the edge; it cannot hold and update leaderboard data structures.",null],2],["x41",0,3,"Users update their profiles and often read them again straight away. The cache in front of the database must not return a stale profile after an update. Which caching strategy fits best?",["Write-through, updating the cache every time the database is written","Lazy loading with a long TTL on every cached profile","Lazy loading alone, filling the cache only on a cache miss","Caching all profiles at startup and never expiring them"],0,"Write-through updates the cache as part of each write, so a read right after an update gets the new value. Lazy loading only fills the cache on a miss, so an existing cached entry can stay stale until it expires.","Domain 3: Lazy loading vs write-through",[null,"A long TTL keeps outdated entries in the cache even longer after an update, making stale reads more likely.","Lazy loading only writes to the cache on a miss, so an old cached profile stays until it expires.","Never expiring a startup snapshot guarantees stale data as soon as any profile changes."],2],["x42",0,3,"A CloudFront distribution has a low cache hit ratio. The investigation shows that all query strings, cookies and headers are included in the cache key, though the origin only varies content on one query string. What should the architect do?",["Enable Origin Shield in the Region nearest to the origin","Use a cache policy whose cache key includes only that one query string","Lower the default TTL so that objects refresh more often","Put AWS Global Accelerator in front of the distribution"],1,"Including unneeded values in the cache key creates many separate cache entries for the same content; a cache policy with only the query string that matters lets requests share entries. Origin Shield adds a central caching layer that reduces origin load but does not fix the fragmented cache key.","Domain 3: CloudFront cache keys and TTLs",["Origin Shield reduces load on the origin but does not fix a fragmented cache key at the edge.",null,"A lower TTL makes objects expire sooner, which lowers the cache hit ratio further.","Global Accelerator improves network paths but does not cache, so it cannot raise a CloudFront hit ratio."],3],["x43",0,3,"A social networking app must answer queries such as 'which friends of my friends like this band' with low latency across billions of relationships. Which database fits best?",["Amazon DocumentDB","Amazon Redshift","Amazon Neptune","Amazon DynamoDB"],2,"Neptune is a managed graph database built for traversing highly connected data such as social relationships. DynamoDB can store the items, but multi-hop relationship queries would need many lookups and complex application code.","Domain 3: Choosing a database (Neptune)",["DocumentDB stores JSON documents; multi-hop relationship queries are not what it is designed for.","Redshift is a data warehouse for analytics, not a low-latency store for relationship traversal.",null,"DynamoDB would need many chained lookups and application code to walk friend-of-friend relationships."],1],["x44",0,3,"On-premises applications write files over NFS, and the company wants those files stored as objects in Amazon S3 while recently used data stays in a local cache for low-latency access. What should the architect deploy?",["AWS Storage Gateway Tape Gateway","AWS DataSync with a scheduled task","AWS Storage Gateway Volume Gateway","Amazon S3 File Gateway"],3,"S3 File Gateway presents NFS or SMB shares on premises, stores files as S3 objects and keeps a local cache of recent data. Volume Gateway exposes iSCSI block volumes, not file shares whose files appear as S3 objects.","Domain 3: Storage Gateway",["Tape Gateway presents a virtual tape library for backup software, not NFS file shares.","DataSync copies data in scheduled transfers and gives applications no live NFS mount with a local cache.","Volume Gateway exposes iSCSI block volumes, so files are not stored as individual S3 objects.",null],1],["x45",0,3,"A DynamoDB table uses the current date as its partition key. Every write for the day lands on the same key and requests are throttled, even though the table's total capacity is far from used. What is the best fix?",["Spread writes by adding a calculated or random suffix to the partition key","Switch the table from provisioned capacity to on-demand capacity","Raise the provisioned write capacity of the table","Add DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) in front of the table"],0,"Throttling while total capacity is unused points to a hot partition; a higher-cardinality key, such as the date plus a suffix, spreads writes across partitions. On-demand mode removes table-level capacity planning, but each partition still has throughput limits, so a single hot key keeps throttling.","Domain 3: DynamoDB partition key design",[null,"On-demand mode removes table-level capacity planning, but per-partition limits still throttle a single hot key.","More table capacity does not help when every write targets one partition key.","DAX caches reads; it does not spread writes, which is where the throttling happens."],3],["x46",0,3,"An existing DynamoDB table uses customerId as its partition key. The team now needs to query orders efficiently by productId, a different attribute. What should the architect add?",["A local secondary index with productId as its sort key","A global secondary index keyed on productId","A Scan operation with a filter expression on productId","DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) to cache the results"],1,"A global secondary index can use a different partition key and can be added to an existing table. A local secondary index must keep the table's partition key and can only be created when the table is created, so it cannot serve this need.","Domain 3: DynamoDB secondary indexes",["An LSI must keep the table's partition key and can only be created with the table, so it cannot be added now.",null,"A Scan reads the whole table and filters afterwards, which is slow and uses a lot of capacity.","DAX caches reads of existing access patterns; it does not add a new way to query by productId."],2],["x47",0,3,"A company runs Apache Kafka on premises with many producer and consumer applications written against Kafka client libraries. It wants a managed service on AWS with as few code changes as possible. What should the architect use?",["Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon Data Firehose","Amazon MSK","Amazon SQS FIFO queues"],2,"Amazon MSK runs Apache Kafka as a managed service, so existing Kafka clients connect with little or no change. Kinesis Data Streams is a similar streaming service but uses its own API, so the applications would have to be rewritten.","Domain 3: Kinesis Data Streams vs Firehose vs MSK",["Kinesis Data Streams uses its own API, so every Kafka producer and consumer would need rewriting.","Firehose delivers streams to destinations; it does not speak the Kafka protocol for existing clients.",null,"SQS is a message queue with a different API and model, so the Kafka applications would need rewriting."],1],["x48",0,3,"A data lake in S3 is queried with Athena and Redshift Spectrum. The company needs central, fine-grained permissions, such as hiding salary columns from some analysts, managed in one place. What should the architect use?",["S3 bucket policies with a separate prefix for each column","Amazon Macie sensitive data discovery jobs","AWS Glue crawlers that skip the sensitive columns","AWS Lake Formation column-level permissions"],3,"Lake Formation provides central table, column and row-level permissions that integrated services such as Athena and Redshift Spectrum enforce. Bucket policies work on objects and prefixes, so they cannot hide individual columns inside a file.","Domain 3: Analytics services (Lake Formation)",["Bucket policies control objects and prefixes; they cannot hide individual columns inside a data file.","Macie discovers sensitive data but does not enforce column permissions for Athena or Redshift Spectrum.","Crawlers build catalog tables; leaving columns out of the catalog is not access control and hides them from everyone.",null],2],["x49",0,4,"A fault-tolerant batch workload runs on Spot Instances in an Auto Scaling group. The company wants to lower the chance of interruptions while still getting low prices. How should the group be configured?",["Use many instance types across several AZs with the price-capacity-optimized allocation strategy","Use a single instance type in one AZ with the lowest-price allocation strategy","Use the lowest-price strategy and set the Spot maximum price to the On-Demand price","Use one instance type and request replacements manually after each interruption"],0,"Diversifying across instance types and AZs with price-capacity-optimized picks the Spot pools with the most spare capacity at a low price, which reduces interruptions. The lowest-price strategy focuses on the cheapest pools, which are often the ones most likely to be interrupted.","Domain 4: Spot allocation strategies",[null,"One instance type in one AZ gives a single capacity pool, raising the chance of interruptions.","A maximum price does not reduce interruptions caused by capacity being reclaimed, and lowest-price favors crowded pools.","Manual replacement adds delay and work, and a single instance type keeps interruption risk high."],2],["x50",0,4,"A web tier needs a baseline of four instances that must always be available, and extra capacity during peaks that can tolerate interruption. What is the most cost-effective Auto Scaling group setup?",["Only Spot Instances, with capacity rebalancing turned on","A mixed instances policy with an On-Demand base of four and Spot above it","Only On-Demand Instances, with a scheduled scaling action","Dedicated Hosts sized for the peak load"],1,"A mixed instances policy lets the On-Demand base cover the must-stay baseline (which can be further discounted with Savings Plans) while Spot adds cheap peak capacity. An all-Spot group risks losing the baseline instances when capacity is reclaimed.","Domain 4: Mixed-instances Auto Scaling groups",["An all-Spot group can lose its baseline instances when capacity is reclaimed, breaking the always-available need.",null,"All On-Demand pays full price for peak capacity that could run on cheaper Spot Instances.","Dedicated Hosts sized for peak are the most expensive choice and idle most of the time."],2],["x51",0,4,"A company runs Linux-based Java microservices on x86 EC2 instances and wants better price performance with little development effort. What should the architect recommend first?",["Move the services to Dedicated Instances","Move to larger x86 instances and consolidate the services","Test and move to AWS Graviton-based instances","Move the services to EC2 Mac instances"],2,"Graviton instances are Arm-based and usually give better price performance; Java runs on Arm JVMs, so the move mostly needs testing and new builds of native dependencies. Dedicated Instances add cost for hardware isolation and do nothing for price performance.","Domain 4: Right-sizing and Graviton",["Dedicated Instances add cost for hardware isolation and do nothing for price performance.","Bigger x86 instances raise cost and do not improve the price-performance ratio.",null,"Mac instances are for building Apple software and are an expensive, poor fit for Linux services."],2],["x52",0,4,"A company will run m6i instances in us-east-1 steadily for three years and has no plans to change instance family or Region. It wants the largest discount while still being able to change instance size and operating system. Which option fits?",["A Compute Savings Plan with a three-year term","Spot Instances covered by a capacity reservation","Dedicated Hosts with a three-year reservation","An EC2 Instance Savings Plan for the m6i family in us-east-1"],3,"EC2 Instance Savings Plans commit to one instance family in one Region in exchange for a deeper discount than Compute Savings Plans, and still allow size and OS changes within that family. A Compute Savings Plan is more flexible, but that flexibility, which this company does not need, comes with a smaller discount.","Domain 4: EC2 purchase options and Savings Plans",["A Compute Savings Plan is more flexible but gives a smaller discount, and the company needs none of that flexibility.","Spot Instances can be interrupted and are not a commitment discount for steady three-year use.","Dedicated Hosts are for licensing or compliance needs and cost more than shared-tenancy instances with a plan.",null],3],["x53",0,4,"A research institute shares a large public data set in S3 with other organizations and wants the people who download it to pay for the requests and data transfer. What should the architect enable?",["Requester Pays on the bucket","CloudFront signed URLs for each downloader","Presigned URLs that expire after one day","S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket"],0,"With Requester Pays, the requester's AWS account is charged for requests and data downloaded, while the bucket owner still pays for storage. Signed or presigned URLs control who can download, but the bucket owner still pays for the transfer.","Domain 4: S3 Requester Pays",[null,"CloudFront signed URLs control who can download, but the owner still pays for the delivery.","Presigned URLs grant temporary access but leave the bucket owner paying for requests and transfer.","Transfer Acceleration adds charges for faster transfers; it does not shift costs to downloaders."],1],["x54",0,4,"A company with many accounts wants organization-wide dashboards of S3 storage and activity, including recommendations such as finding buckets with incomplete multipart uploads. What should the architect use?",["S3 Inventory reports on each bucket","Amazon S3 Storage Lens","AWS Cost Explorer grouped by service","CloudTrail data events for S3"],1,"S3 Storage Lens gives organization-wide usage and activity metrics with recommendations to cut costs and apply best practices. S3 Inventory lists objects in a single bucket but does not provide cross-account dashboards or recommendations.","Domain 4: S3 Storage Lens",["S3 Inventory lists objects in one bucket; it has no organization-wide dashboards or recommendations.",null,"Cost Explorer shows spend by service but not storage metrics such as incomplete multipart uploads.","CloudTrail data events log individual requests and add cost; they are not a storage analytics dashboard."],1],["x55",0,4,"A team takes EBS snapshots by hand. It wants daily snapshots kept for seven days, plus monthly snapshots kept for years that are rarely restored, at the lowest cost and with the least effort. What should the architect use?",["Keep every snapshot in the standard tier and delete old ones by script","Copy each snapshot into S3 Glacier Deep Archive with a lifecycle rule","Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager policies, moving monthly snapshots to the archive tier","Detach volumes each month and keep them as unattached volumes"],2,"Data Lifecycle Manager automates creation and retention of snapshots, and EBS Snapshots Archive offers much lower storage cost for rarely restored long-term snapshots. Snapshots cannot be copied into a Glacier storage class by an S3 lifecycle rule, because they are not objects in a bucket you own.","Domain 4: EBS snapshot costs and Data Lifecycle Manager",["Keeping long-term snapshots in the standard tier costs more, and custom scripts add ongoing work.","EBS snapshots are not objects in your bucket, so S3 lifecycle rules cannot move them to Deep Archive.",null,"Unattached volumes cost far more than snapshots and give no automated retention."],2],["x56",0,4,"Cost Explorer shows EBS spending rising even though many EC2 instances were terminated months ago. What is the most likely cause, and the fix?",["Terminated instances still bill for compute; buy Reserved Instances for them","The gp2 volumes are billed at a higher rate; convert them all to gp3","Stopped instances bill for EBS; terminate every stopped instance now","Volumes left in the available state; snapshot if needed, then delete them"],3,"Volumes whose DeleteOnTermination flag was off stay behind in the available state and keep billing for storage; they should be backed up if required and deleted. Converting to gp3 lowers the rate but still pays for volumes nobody uses.","Domain 4: Unattached EBS volumes",["Terminated instances stop billing for compute; buying Reserved Instances for them makes no sense.","gp3 is cheaper, but converting still pays for volumes nothing uses; the orphaned volumes are the real waste.","Stopped instances do bill for their EBS volumes, but the scenario is about volumes left behind by terminated instances.",null],2],["x57",0,4,"To save money during a month-long project pause, a team stops an Amazon RDS instance. A week later, they find it running and billing again. What explains this, and what is the better approach?",["RDS restarts a stopped instance after seven days; snapshot and delete it, then restore later","Stopped RDS instances bill fully for compute; switch the instance to Multi-AZ","Someone else must have started it; add an SCP that denies rds:StartDBInstance","RDS instances cannot be stopped at all; move the database to DynamoDB"],0,"RDS automatically starts a stopped DB instance after seven days, so for a long pause it is cheaper to take a final snapshot, delete the instance and restore when needed. Blaming another user and blocking StartDBInstance does not stop the automatic restart, which is AWS's own behavior.","Domain 4: Stopping idle databases",[null,"A stopped instance does not bill for instance hours, and Multi-AZ would double the cost.","The restart is AWS's own automatic behavior after seven days, so blocking StartDBInstance does not stop it.","RDS instances can be stopped temporarily; moving to DynamoDB is a redesign, not a fix."],3],["x58",0,4,"Large software downloads are served straight from EC2 instances to users around the world, and internet data transfer out is the biggest line on the bill. What change most reduces cost and load on the instances?",["Put AWS Global Accelerator in front of the instances","Serve the downloads through Amazon CloudFront","Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the download bucket","Move the downloads to larger, network-optimized instances"],1,"CloudFront caches downloads at edge locations, data transfer from AWS origins to CloudFront is not charged, and CloudFront delivery is typically cheaper than direct internet egress. Global Accelerator improves network paths but does not cache anything and adds its own charges.","Domain 4: Data transfer costs and CloudFront",["Global Accelerator improves routing but does not cache content, and it adds its own fees.",null,"Transfer Acceleration speeds transfers to and from S3 at extra cost; it does not reduce egress from EC2.","Bigger instances raise compute cost and leave every byte still going out over the internet."],2],["x59",0,4,"Finance needs hourly, line-item detail of every AWS charge, including resource IDs and tags, loaded into Athena for its own reports. What should the architect set up?",["Saved reports in AWS Cost Explorer","AWS Budgets with daily email alerts","The Cost and Usage Report delivered to S3","Cost optimization checks in AWS Trusted Advisor"],2,"The Cost and Usage Report delivers the most detailed billing data, down to hourly line items with resource IDs and tags, to S3, where Athena can query it. Cost Explorer shows summarized views and charts but is not a line-item data feed.","Domain 4: Cost visibility tools",["Cost Explorer shows summarized charts and views; it is not an hourly line-item data feed for Athena.","Budgets send alerts on thresholds; they do not export detailed billing records.",null,"Trusted Advisor gives optimization recommendations, not detailed billing line items."],1],["x60",0,4,"Account A in an AWS Organization bought Reserved Instances that it now uses only partly. Account B in the same organization runs matching instances in the same Region at On-Demand rates. What happens to billing?",["Nothing, because Reserved Instances apply only in the account that bought them","Account A must formally transfer the Reserved Instances to account B","The unused Reserved Instances must be sold on the Reserved Instance Marketplace","Account B's usage gets the unused RI discount by default"],3,"With consolidated billing, Reserved Instance and Savings Plans discounts are shared across the organization's accounts by default, so unused RI hours apply to matching usage in account B. Discount sharing can be disabled, but no transfer between accounts is needed.","Domain 4: Consolidated billing and RI sharing",["Under consolidated billing, RI discounts are shared across accounts by default, not locked to the buyer.","No transfer is needed; the discount applies automatically to matching usage in other accounts.","Selling is unnecessary when matching usage elsewhere in the organization can use the discount.",null],3]]);
